WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.230
--Fourth annual James R. Killian
Junior Faculty Achievement

00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:04.900
Award Lecture.

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:07.950
I'm Krishna Rajagopal,
chair of the faculty.

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:12.650
Before we begin, just
to tell you the program,

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:16.620
it's my guess that the
lecture and the Q&amp;A

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:21.770
afterwards will go until about
5:10, 5:15 along those lines.

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:24.230
And then afterwards there
will be a reception downstairs

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:26.146
in the Bush Room to which
everyone is invited.

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:31.280
The James R. Killian Junior
Faculty Achievement Award

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:34.130
was established in
the spring of 1971

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:36.740
as a permanent tribute
to Dr. James Killian, who

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:40.280
was president of MIT
from 1948 to '59,

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:45.020
and chairman of the corporation
from then until 1971.

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:47.420
The purpose of the
award is to recognize

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:50.490
extraordinary professional
achievement by MIT faculty

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:54.590
members and to communicate
their accomplishments to members

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:57.470
of the Institute writ large.

00:00:57.470 --> 00:01:00.680
The title of Killian
Lecturer is the highest honor

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:06.020
that the MIT faculty may
bestow on one of its own.

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:08.190
It is our privilege today
to honor and welcome

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:11.350
Tyler Jacks, the David H.
Koch Professor of Biology

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:14.460
and the director of the Koch
Institute-- the Koch Institute

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:17.450
for Integrative Cancer
Research at MIT.

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:20.780
As noted by his colleagues on
the Killian award committee,

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:23.770
professor Jacks has long been
a pioneer in the MIT cancer

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:26.160
research community.

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:29.000
His early achievements in
genetically engineering mouse

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:31.330
models that closely
replicate the human disease

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:33.600
allowed certain types
of tumors to be studied

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.220
with greater reliability.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:36.700
And today the
models he developed

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:39.530
are used in labs worldwide,
contributing to a better

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:42.070
understanding of disease
pathogenesis and progression,

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:47.000
and enabling the testing of
novel anticancer strategies.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:49.730
Both in and out of the
lab, professor Jacks

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:53.220
is described by colleagues
as bold and visionary.

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:55.090
His nominators point
out that it takes

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:57.530
a village of passionate
and dedicated people

00:01:57.530 --> 00:01:59.830
to invent solutions for
the many cancers that

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:02.820
afflict our society,
and they point out

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:07.590
this is exactly what he has
created over in building 76.

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:10.479
Yet as one colleague commented,
"successes like the Koch

00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:13.090
Institute do not just happen.

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:15.400
They are created through
dynamic leadership."

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:18.440
And this is one of the qualities
for which we honor and thank

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:21.320
Professor Jacks today.

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:24.520
Since 2001 when he was selected
to direct the center that

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:26.470
would become the
Koch Institute, he

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:28.920
has foster
transformative changes

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.060
by cultivating
multi-disciplinary

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:35.260
collaboration between cancer
biologists and engineers.

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:38.150
Today the Koch Institute houses
dozens of faculty research

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:39.870
groups and serves
as an organizing

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:44.220
body for more than 500
researchers at MIT.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:47.050
Faculty and student cite his
mentorship and his commitment

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:49.540
to bringing the different
cultures and languages

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:52.800
and research agendas of these
two communities, these two

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.270
worlds together.

00:02:55.270 --> 00:02:57.530
He also does a marvelous
job of reaching out

00:02:57.530 --> 00:03:01.710
to the larger world outside
MIT, communicating challenges

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:04.340
over common lessons learned at
the cancer research frontier

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:05.850
to broad audiences.

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:08.410
For example, and I can
personally recommend this,

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:11.220
through a TEDx Cambridge
talk that he gave back

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:14.650
in September of 2014.

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:16.620
Professor Jacks
joined the MIT faculty

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:19.650
in 1992, and in the years
since he's been recognized

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:21.660
with numerous awards.

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:23.540
He's been named a Howard
Hughes Investigator

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:27.330
and received the Paul Marks
prize for cancer research.

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:29.440
He was elected to two
of the nation's most

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:31.880
prestigious academies, the
National Academy of Sciences

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:35.310
and the Institute
of Medicine in 2009.

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:37.960
That same year he was elected
president of the American

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:40.650
Association of Cancer
Research and he

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:43.280
was appointed chair of the
National Cancer Institute's

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:46.760
National Cancer advisory
board in 2011, and in 2012

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:50.000
was elected to the American
Academy of Arts and Sciences.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:51.560
So it's a privilege
and a pleasure

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:53.370
to welcome Tyler
Jacks to the lectern.

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:55.342
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:55.342 --> 00:03:56.821
Thank you so much.

00:03:56.821 --> 00:03:59.286
And now I get to actually
give you the certificate.

00:03:59.286 --> 00:04:00.765
I have the mic for
another second.

00:04:00.765 --> 00:04:02.737
OK.

00:04:02.737 --> 00:04:06.188
[APPLAUSE]

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:23.980
So Tyler, it is my privilege
to represent the MIT faculty

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:26.750
in honoring you with the James
R. Killian Faculty Achievement

00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:28.620
Award, through
which we recognize

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:31.590
your influence on the field
of cancer research achieved

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:33.490
through your own
pioneering work,

00:04:33.490 --> 00:04:36.680
and your visionary leadership
to unite MIT's cancer biologists

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:40.540
and engineers in a collaborative
multi-disciplinary research

00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:41.540
community.

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:43.019
Congratulations.

00:04:43.019 --> 00:04:46.470
[APPLAUSE]

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:59.300
Well, thanks Krishna for that
very, very nice and generous

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:00.600
introduction.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.360
At the appropriate
time I'm happy for you

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:03.616
to deliver my eulogy.

00:05:03.616 --> 00:05:04.926
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:07.750 --> 00:05:11.450
I want to thank Professor
Rigobon and the Killian award

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:15.570
committee for selecting
me for this honor.

00:05:15.570 --> 00:05:19.830
It's a tremendous honor I
must say, in part because it's

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:22.830
given to me by my
peers, and in part

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:27.650
because it represents the great
tradition of our university.

00:05:27.650 --> 00:05:30.980
I'm frankly blown away to see
my name on the list of Killian

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:33.160
award recipients.

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:35.320
I'm not sure I
belong there, but I'm

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:38.820
happy to be there just the same.

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:42.590
This award is particularly
meaningful to me

00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:46.350
because I have an extremely
long history at MIT.

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:50.960
I came here in 1988 to pursue
my post-doctoral studies

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:53.520
with Bob Weinberg in
the Whitehead Institute,

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:57.110
and joined the faculty in 1992.

00:05:57.110 --> 00:05:59.830
So that's a span in
total of 28 years.

00:05:59.830 --> 00:06:00.820
Quite remarkable.

00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:03.300
Of course I started when
I was nine, so that's OK.

00:06:06.250 --> 00:06:12.470
My brother is a graduate of
this institute, class of 1976.

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:15.120
And some of you know that my
father was a faculty member

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:18.260
here for 30 years,
starting in the late 1950s

00:06:18.260 --> 00:06:21.690
until his retirement
in the 1980s.

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:23.949
Rumor has it that I was
conceived on the MIT campus.

00:06:23.949 --> 00:06:24.990
I'm not sure that's true.

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:26.385
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:27.315 --> 00:06:29.435
A story for another time.

00:06:29.435 --> 00:06:31.135
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:31.135 --> 00:06:34.130
But my dad used to bring
me to MIT when I was a kid.

00:06:34.130 --> 00:06:36.460
And I can tell you that
Kendall Square in the 1960s

00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:38.920
was a very different place.

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:41.060
There was literally
one place to eat.

00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:42.260
It was the F&amp;T Diner.

00:06:42.260 --> 00:06:45.310
Some of the old timers will
remember the F&amp;T Diner.

00:06:45.310 --> 00:06:46.840
It's where the T station is.

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:49.040
And my dad and I would
go there each time,

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:51.142
and we would always have
pastrami sandwiches.

00:06:51.142 --> 00:06:53.600
And I still remember the taste
of those pastrami sandwiches

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:56.110
today.

00:06:56.110 --> 00:06:59.440
And my education from MIT
started very early as well.

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:02.640
I learned to play poker
from my father's colleagues

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:05.140
around the table at our house,
and their monthly poker game.

00:07:05.140 --> 00:07:08.730
And that included people like
Frank Fisher and Bill Pounds

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:12.810
and Peter Gill and
Howard Johnson actually

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:15.620
used to come-- Howard Johnson,
former president of MIT.

00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:17.250
That wasn't a
simpler time, Rafael,

00:07:17.250 --> 00:07:20.060
when the president could gamble
with members of the faculty.

00:07:20.060 --> 00:07:21.410
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:24.564
My dad passed away
about 15 years ago,

00:07:24.564 --> 00:07:26.230
but I'm quite sure
he would be extremely

00:07:26.230 --> 00:07:29.480
proud of today's events.

00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:32.210
I want to thank
you all for coming.

00:07:32.210 --> 00:07:34.970
I know that you expected a
lecture on gravitational waves,

00:07:34.970 --> 00:07:35.470
but I'm--

00:07:35.470 --> 00:07:36.660
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:40.050
--glad your here anyway.

00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:41.770
I want to thank
the Koch Institute

00:07:41.770 --> 00:07:44.520
folks who gave me a rousing
send-off from the building much

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:45.270
to my surprise.

00:07:45.270 --> 00:07:49.030
I walked out the door, there
were 200 people in a gauntlet,

00:07:49.030 --> 00:07:51.450
lining the courtyard as I
walked my way over here.

00:07:51.450 --> 00:07:52.340
So thanks for that.

00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:54.490
That was really
quite a send-off.

00:07:54.490 --> 00:07:57.140
I want to thank David
Koch for making the time

00:07:57.140 --> 00:07:59.880
to come up from New York
to be with us today.

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:01.970
I want to give a special
welcome to my wife

00:08:01.970 --> 00:08:04.390
Laura and my daughter
Libby, and thanks to them

00:08:04.390 --> 00:08:09.000
for their never-ending
love and support.

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:11.370
And I want to make a
special thanks to Joe Cohen.

00:08:11.370 --> 00:08:12.555
Where is Joe?

00:08:12.555 --> 00:08:14.740
I hope he's here.

00:08:14.740 --> 00:08:17.550
Joe Cohen was responsible
for all logistics

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:20.180
related to today's event,
including the advertisement.

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:22.765
And Joe advertised the
hell out of this thing.

00:08:22.765 --> 00:08:24.100
[LAUGHTER]

00:08:24.100 --> 00:08:28.680
I couldn't turn a corner on
campus without seeing my face.

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:31.510
I know what it's like now to be
one of America's Most Wanted.

00:08:34.230 --> 00:08:36.970
So thank you for all
of those things things.

00:08:36.970 --> 00:08:39.940
And certainly thank you
for the recognition.

00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:42.320
I'm being recognized
for two things I guess.

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:44.430
They were mentioned in
Krishna's introduction.

00:08:44.430 --> 00:08:46.740
One is the science
that we've performed

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:48.890
and the other is the Koch
Institute development.

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:51.140
And I'm going to talk a bit
about both of those things

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:52.000
today.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:53.500
With respect to the
science, I think

00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:56.280
it's important to point out that
scientific achievement is not

00:08:56.280 --> 00:08:57.370
a singular activity.

00:08:57.370 --> 00:08:59.630
It's the collective
activity of many people.

00:08:59.630 --> 00:09:04.110
And I've been extremely lucky
to attract outstanding people

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:07.680
to work for me over the
last quarter century--

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:10.190
graduate students
like those shown here.

00:09:10.190 --> 00:09:13.430
The names and faces of the past
and present graduate students

00:09:13.430 --> 00:09:15.050
from my group are shown.

00:09:15.050 --> 00:09:18.060
We get wonderful students
at MIT, undergraduates

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:21.340
and graduate students and they
contribute mightily to the work

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:23.410
that we perform in
our laboratories.

00:09:23.410 --> 00:09:25.740
Likewise I've had the
privilege of training

00:09:25.740 --> 00:09:28.480
a very large number of
outstanding postdoctoral

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:29.310
fellows.

00:09:29.310 --> 00:09:31.520
Again the names and
faces of the individuals

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:33.620
presently in the lab and
previously in the lab

00:09:33.620 --> 00:09:34.850
are shown here.

00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:37.700
And these folks, the post-docs
and the graduate students

00:09:37.700 --> 00:09:40.280
have continued to
do great research

00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:43.500
in their independent training
and independent careers,

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:46.320
contributing to discoveries
in cancer research

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:49.119
around the world,
across the country,

00:09:49.119 --> 00:09:50.410
really carrying things forward.

00:09:50.410 --> 00:09:51.920
And I would say
one of my proudest

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:54.660
if not proudest achievements
as a faculty member

00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:57.430
is being able to train
these wonderful people.

00:09:57.430 --> 00:10:01.650
This is the greatest product
of our laboratory's research.

00:10:01.650 --> 00:10:04.220
Students in the post-docs
often get the headlines,

00:10:04.220 --> 00:10:07.650
but importantly they are
assisted by technical help,

00:10:07.650 --> 00:10:09.320
technical assistance.

00:10:09.320 --> 00:10:11.510
We've worked with a number
of great technicians

00:10:11.510 --> 00:10:12.250
over the years.

00:10:12.250 --> 00:10:14.360
Their names and
faces are shown here.

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:17.500
They deserve a lot of
the credit as well.

00:10:17.500 --> 00:10:20.890
And over the years we've
had wonderful undergraduate

00:10:20.890 --> 00:10:23.180
students and summer students.

00:10:23.180 --> 00:10:25.170
High school students
work with us.

00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:28.620
And they contribute as
well, both in the science

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:31.780
that they pursue,
and also as mentees

00:10:31.780 --> 00:10:33.610
for the mentors in
the lab so that they

00:10:33.610 --> 00:10:37.560
can learn how to
become better teachers,

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:41.280
and to learn the
skills of mentorship.

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:45.690
And finally, the lab runs
because of great people

00:10:45.690 --> 00:10:48.280
who helped administer the lab.

00:10:48.280 --> 00:10:50.400
And I've been assisted
by wonderful people

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:51.700
over the years.

00:10:51.700 --> 00:10:56.250
Shown here, my administrative
assistants and lab managers.

00:10:56.250 --> 00:10:59.130
I want to make special
reference to Judy Teixeira who's

00:10:59.130 --> 00:11:01.260
sitting in the second row here.

00:11:01.260 --> 00:11:04.730
Judy's been with me for 15 years
as my administrative assistant.

00:11:04.730 --> 00:11:07.850
The smirk on her
face sort of suggests

00:11:07.850 --> 00:11:09.930
that she's tired of
hearing yet one more thing

00:11:09.930 --> 00:11:11.540
that I need her to do.

00:11:11.540 --> 00:11:14.260
But thank you Judy for all that
you've done over the years.

00:11:14.260 --> 00:11:17.840
Now, the introduction
referenced the development

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:18.760
of the Koch Institute.

00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:20.301
And the development
of Koch Institute

00:11:20.301 --> 00:11:22.120
likewise was a
shared experience.

00:11:22.120 --> 00:11:26.660
It was a joint effort,
involving many, many people,

00:11:26.660 --> 00:11:31.280
including very importantly
Jackie Lees, who's

00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:33.590
the associate director
of the Koch Institute

00:11:33.590 --> 00:11:35.480
and was the associate
director of the Center

00:11:35.480 --> 00:11:38.290
for Cancer Research
starting back in 2001.

00:11:38.290 --> 00:11:39.830
And Jackie and I
really conceived

00:11:39.830 --> 00:11:42.570
of changing the course
of cancer research

00:11:42.570 --> 00:11:44.640
and including the
engineering community

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:46.010
in that new direction.

00:11:46.010 --> 00:11:48.360
We were joined at
that time, 2007,

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:52.480
by Dane Wittrup, who likewise
made major contributions

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:54.180
to the directions
that we finally took

00:11:54.180 --> 00:11:56.160
and the planning that
we made at that time.

00:11:56.160 --> 00:11:58.410
Phil Sharp was a
major force in that.

00:11:58.410 --> 00:12:01.560
Bob Langer, Doug Lauffenburger,
and many others on our factory

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:05.070
contributed significantly
to the progress and planning

00:12:05.070 --> 00:12:06.590
that we made.

00:12:06.590 --> 00:12:09.600
Cindy Quense is the assistant
director for administration

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:12.520
of the Koch and does a wonderful
job overseeing the headquarters

00:12:12.520 --> 00:12:14.330
staff and the operations.

00:12:14.330 --> 00:12:16.400
Anne Deconinck and
before her Robert Urban

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:18.660
have served as executive
directors of the Koch,

00:12:18.660 --> 00:12:21.630
taking care of many of the
outstanding outreach efforts

00:12:21.630 --> 00:12:23.470
that we make in
various directions.

00:12:23.470 --> 00:12:25.095
So I'm very indebted
to all of them

00:12:25.095 --> 00:12:27.220
and many others who've made
the Koch Institute what

00:12:27.220 --> 00:12:29.750
it is today.

00:12:29.750 --> 00:12:31.850
This wouldn't have
happened if not

00:12:31.850 --> 00:12:34.620
for the support
and encouragement

00:12:34.620 --> 00:12:37.650
and massive effort by
the MIT administration.

00:12:37.650 --> 00:12:41.200
This happened in 2007 while
Susan Hockfield was president.

00:12:41.200 --> 00:12:43.470
Rafael Reif was provost.

00:12:43.470 --> 00:12:46.390
And they too
contributed to the idea

00:12:46.390 --> 00:12:48.420
of bringing scientists
and engineers together

00:12:48.420 --> 00:12:49.430
under one roof.

00:12:49.430 --> 00:12:51.970
If not for them we
wouldn't have done it.

00:12:51.970 --> 00:12:54.790
And they continue to be
tremendous supporters--

00:12:54.790 --> 00:12:56.910
Susan whose office is now
in the Koch Institute.

00:12:56.910 --> 00:13:00.620
Raphael as president has
remained a steadfast supporter

00:13:00.620 --> 00:13:01.580
of the Koch Institute.

00:13:01.580 --> 00:13:04.210
So I'm very grateful to
the MIT administration

00:13:04.210 --> 00:13:06.032
for helping us do this.

00:13:06.032 --> 00:13:07.490
I'm grateful to
the deans that I've

00:13:07.490 --> 00:13:10.060
worked with over the years
in science and engineering,

00:13:10.060 --> 00:13:12.820
vice presidents for research,
Claude Canizares, Maria

00:13:12.820 --> 00:13:16.360
Zuber who've been
tremendously supportive.

00:13:16.360 --> 00:13:20.060
And we wouldn't be here if not
for one specific individual,

00:13:20.060 --> 00:13:21.370
and that's David Koch.

00:13:21.370 --> 00:13:24.520
David was remarkably
generous in providing

00:13:24.520 --> 00:13:26.940
the funds necessary for
us to build the building

00:13:26.940 --> 00:13:29.530
that we now occupy,
to provide funds

00:13:29.530 --> 00:13:32.560
to equip the building
that we now use,

00:13:32.560 --> 00:13:34.970
and has also funded six
professorships along the way.

00:13:34.970 --> 00:13:37.740
So David has been
remarkably generous to us

00:13:37.740 --> 00:13:40.510
here at MIT for cancer
research, and frankly

00:13:40.510 --> 00:13:42.620
around the country in
support of cancer research.

00:13:42.620 --> 00:13:45.460
So thank you to you David.

00:13:45.460 --> 00:13:47.940
The idea of doing cancer
research in the way

00:13:47.940 --> 00:13:50.650
that we now do it in
the Koch Institute

00:13:50.650 --> 00:13:52.540
takes advantage
of great strengths

00:13:52.540 --> 00:13:55.860
that we have, somewhat
uniquely here at MIT.

00:13:55.860 --> 00:13:58.040
Cancer scientists
working together

00:13:58.040 --> 00:14:02.390
with cancer-oriented engineers
to encourage new approaches

00:14:02.390 --> 00:14:04.510
to longstanding
problems in cancer.

00:14:04.510 --> 00:14:06.490
The idea was that there
would be opportunities

00:14:06.490 --> 00:14:10.260
at the interfaces of various
disciplines in basic science

00:14:10.260 --> 00:14:12.865
and engineering to approach
problems in cancer that

00:14:12.865 --> 00:14:15.030
have been very
difficult using singular

00:14:15.030 --> 00:14:16.790
approaches in the
past-- that there was

00:14:16.790 --> 00:14:20.200
some magic at this interface,
and that we could therefore

00:14:20.200 --> 00:14:22.980
make new discoveries,
different kinds of discoveries

00:14:22.980 --> 00:14:25.470
and new inventions
and new technologies

00:14:25.470 --> 00:14:28.480
through a very deliberate
effort in collaboration,

00:14:28.480 --> 00:14:30.920
working across disciplines
to take advantage

00:14:30.920 --> 00:14:33.260
of this rather unique
mix of individuals

00:14:33.260 --> 00:14:35.560
that we have in the Koch.

00:14:35.560 --> 00:14:38.770
And moreover that we
could mobilize our efforts

00:14:38.770 --> 00:14:41.630
beyond those discoveries
and new technologies

00:14:41.630 --> 00:14:45.000
and deliver those to
the benefit of patients.

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:47.220
Not to stop at the
discoveries at the bench,

00:14:47.220 --> 00:14:49.120
but actually to translate
those discoveries

00:14:49.120 --> 00:14:51.330
to the benefit of
cancer patients,

00:14:51.330 --> 00:14:54.980
to change the lives of patients,
to change the course of cancer.

00:14:54.980 --> 00:14:57.610
And frankly I'm pleased to say
that we're starting to do that.

00:14:57.610 --> 00:15:00.640
And I'm extremely
proud of that as well.

00:15:00.640 --> 00:15:04.550
So 2007, we started
thinking about how

00:15:04.550 --> 00:15:05.800
to develop the Koch Institute.

00:15:05.800 --> 00:15:07.633
We planned it over the
next couple of years,

00:15:07.633 --> 00:15:09.460
again with contributions
from many people,

00:15:09.460 --> 00:15:13.140
including folks in the
planning office here at MIT.

00:15:13.140 --> 00:15:17.960
And finally in late
2010, early 2011

00:15:17.960 --> 00:15:22.080
we open the doors to the
new Koch Institute building.

00:15:22.080 --> 00:15:25.030
We brought this project
in, David's proud to say,

00:15:25.030 --> 00:15:27.930
under budget and ahead of
schedule-- quite remarkable.

00:15:27.930 --> 00:15:29.840
And it was celebrated,
as you can see here

00:15:29.840 --> 00:15:33.550
on the front page of the
Boston Globe above the fold.

00:15:33.550 --> 00:15:36.760
"A new style of cancer
research coming together,

00:15:36.760 --> 00:15:39.290
bringing individuals
from diverse backgrounds

00:15:39.290 --> 00:15:42.860
together to approach the
problems in a new way."

00:15:42.860 --> 00:15:45.610
I'd like to point out
that in this same page

00:15:45.610 --> 00:15:48.322
that this doctor
who was indicted

00:15:48.322 --> 00:15:50.230
had nothing to do with us.

00:15:50.230 --> 00:15:51.526
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:51.526 --> 00:15:54.112
I'm quite sure that he
was a Harvard doctor.

00:15:54.112 --> 00:15:56.000
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:57.420 --> 00:15:58.410
So we moved in.

00:15:58.410 --> 00:16:02.617
And this is what things look
like in building 76 now.

00:16:02.617 --> 00:16:03.700
It's a beautiful building.

00:16:03.700 --> 00:16:04.491
You've all seen it.

00:16:04.491 --> 00:16:06.370
Some of you work inside of it.

00:16:06.370 --> 00:16:07.850
And around the rim
are the faculty

00:16:07.850 --> 00:16:09.770
who occupy this building.

00:16:09.770 --> 00:16:13.500
In blue writing are the science
faculty and in green writing

00:16:13.500 --> 00:16:15.080
the engineering
faculty-- blurred

00:16:15.080 --> 00:16:17.000
lines between these
disciplines now so

00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:19.030
it isn't appropriate to
use different shading

00:16:19.030 --> 00:16:20.222
for these folks.

00:16:20.222 --> 00:16:21.680
But this is a
remarkable collection

00:16:21.680 --> 00:16:24.750
of people, extremely
talented in their fields

00:16:24.750 --> 00:16:28.250
and also quite distinctive
in their willingness

00:16:28.250 --> 00:16:30.660
to broaden and
engage researchers

00:16:30.660 --> 00:16:33.290
across many different areas
of science and engineering

00:16:33.290 --> 00:16:34.780
and technology development.

00:16:34.780 --> 00:16:36.620
They're very ambitious.

00:16:36.620 --> 00:16:38.140
They're very creative.

00:16:38.140 --> 00:16:40.140
They're are a bit
unconventional.

00:16:40.140 --> 00:16:43.020
Frankly speaking, you don't find
cancer research institutions

00:16:43.020 --> 00:16:45.280
like this anywhere else.

00:16:45.280 --> 00:16:48.290
Cancer research
institutions, in most places

00:16:48.290 --> 00:16:52.910
look like a swim meet, where
everybody's more or less doing

00:16:52.910 --> 00:16:56.820
the same stroke, staying in
their lane, with their heads

00:16:56.820 --> 00:16:58.470
down.

00:16:58.470 --> 00:17:00.550
Cancer research at MIT
and the Koch Institute

00:17:00.550 --> 00:17:02.100
looks quite different.

00:17:02.100 --> 00:17:04.060
Nobody's doing the same stroke.

00:17:04.060 --> 00:17:07.520
Some people are doing
strokes you cannot recognize.

00:17:07.520 --> 00:17:09.829
People are ripping
up the lane markers.

00:17:09.829 --> 00:17:13.109
Angie Belcher is
lighting them on fire.

00:17:13.109 --> 00:17:15.770
Mike Yaffe and Paul Hammond
are diving off the high board

00:17:15.770 --> 00:17:19.352
doing cannon balls
into the water.

00:17:19.352 --> 00:17:22.450
Cancer research in the Koch
Institute looks different.

00:17:22.450 --> 00:17:24.250
It's frankly a frothy mess.

00:17:24.250 --> 00:17:25.708
[LAUGHTER]

00:17:26.680 --> 00:17:28.910
And we like it that way.

00:17:28.910 --> 00:17:31.570
We're doing different things
and we're changing the paradigm.

00:17:31.570 --> 00:17:34.230
We're changing how we're
approaching the cancer problem,

00:17:34.230 --> 00:17:37.720
bringing the inventiveness,
the innovation, the spirit

00:17:37.720 --> 00:17:41.070
and creativity of
MIT together to solve

00:17:41.070 --> 00:17:43.990
these longstanding problems
in a different way.

00:17:43.990 --> 00:17:47.300
Now it's not only about what's
happening inside the building.

00:17:47.300 --> 00:17:50.660
The Koch serves as the National
Cancer Institute designated

00:17:50.660 --> 00:17:52.870
cancer center for MIT.

00:17:52.870 --> 00:17:55.130
And therefore we organize
the cancer research efforts

00:17:55.130 --> 00:17:56.297
on campus.

00:17:56.297 --> 00:17:58.380
And in this way we reach
out to cancer researchers

00:17:58.380 --> 00:17:59.410
across the campus.

00:17:59.410 --> 00:18:02.020
And there's more than
1,000 cancer researchers

00:18:02.020 --> 00:18:04.270
at MIT in fact.

00:18:04.270 --> 00:18:08.560
A full 27 faculty are extramural
members of the Koch Institute.

00:18:08.560 --> 00:18:11.710
It's very much about
collaboration, collaboration

00:18:11.710 --> 00:18:15.460
across disciplines, across
buildings, across the campus.

00:18:15.460 --> 00:18:18.090
And collaboration is very
important to what we do.

00:18:18.090 --> 00:18:20.490
It allows us to take
advantage of the creativity

00:18:20.490 --> 00:18:23.080
of our colleagues independent
of where they come from.

00:18:23.080 --> 00:18:25.290
And there is this
magic at the interface

00:18:25.290 --> 00:18:29.270
if you're open to seeing it,
this collective creativity.

00:18:29.270 --> 00:18:31.350
We see it at play every day.

00:18:31.350 --> 00:18:33.730
And don't discount the
importance of beer--

00:18:33.730 --> 00:18:35.940
extremely important as well.

00:18:35.940 --> 00:18:39.580
The collaborations include the
folks within our communities,

00:18:39.580 --> 00:18:42.530
but importantly, and especially
as we translate our ideas

00:18:42.530 --> 00:18:45.260
out from MIT beyond,
we actually need

00:18:45.260 --> 00:18:47.040
to collaborate
with other people.

00:18:47.040 --> 00:18:49.100
We don't have a cancer
hospital at MIT.

00:18:49.100 --> 00:18:51.350
We don't take care of
cancer patients here.

00:18:51.350 --> 00:18:55.200
And so in order to be able to
do more clinically relevant

00:18:55.200 --> 00:18:56.420
research, we must reach out.

00:18:56.420 --> 00:18:58.920
And we've done that in lots of
different ways, in particular

00:18:58.920 --> 00:19:00.741
with respect to the
Bridge Project, which

00:19:00.741 --> 00:19:02.490
is a project that we
have in collaboration

00:19:02.490 --> 00:19:05.270
with the Dana-Farber
Harvard Cancer Center.

00:19:05.270 --> 00:19:08.550
And here we've funded
teams of investigators.

00:19:08.550 --> 00:19:12.760
Soon that number will be up to
about 25 teams of investigators

00:19:12.760 --> 00:19:16.630
between MIT and Harvard tackling
more clinically proximal

00:19:16.630 --> 00:19:17.950
problems.

00:19:17.950 --> 00:19:20.580
And this again brings our
ideas and our discoveries

00:19:20.580 --> 00:19:22.741
directly to the
benefit of patients.

00:19:22.741 --> 00:19:23.990
We also reach out to industry.

00:19:23.990 --> 00:19:26.570
And these interactions
are also really important.

00:19:26.570 --> 00:19:28.960
There's only so many things
we can do in our labs.

00:19:28.960 --> 00:19:31.770
Sometimes we need the
capital and the expertise

00:19:31.770 --> 00:19:33.200
of people from industry.

00:19:33.200 --> 00:19:36.930
And so we've engaged with
bio-pharmaceutical partners

00:19:36.930 --> 00:19:38.170
in a fairly significant way.

00:19:38.170 --> 00:19:40.684
And we've also spun out
a bunch of companies.

00:19:40.684 --> 00:19:42.600
This is a list of companies
that have spun out

00:19:42.600 --> 00:19:45.590
of the Koch Institute,
intramural and extramural labs,

00:19:45.590 --> 00:19:47.540
only since 2007.

00:19:47.540 --> 00:19:51.760
There's about 42 companies
shown here, amazing to me.

00:19:51.760 --> 00:19:53.441
Bob Langer has
started 35 of them.

00:19:53.441 --> 00:19:53.941
But--

00:19:53.941 --> 00:19:57.790
[LAUGHTER]

00:19:57.790 --> 00:19:59.320
We're very proud of this fact.

00:19:59.320 --> 00:20:02.040
Again, it advances
our work more quickly

00:20:02.040 --> 00:20:05.080
to where it can make
the biggest difference.

00:20:05.080 --> 00:20:09.210
So these kinds of approaches--
organizational structures are

00:20:09.210 --> 00:20:13.050
really important, but
so too is the culture.

00:20:13.050 --> 00:20:15.380
Phil Sharp reminded us
early on that culture

00:20:15.380 --> 00:20:18.510
beats strategy every time,
and he's absolutely right.

00:20:18.510 --> 00:20:20.960
And so we've tried to build
a culture of collaboration

00:20:20.960 --> 00:20:22.120
within the Koch.

00:20:22.120 --> 00:20:24.810
We have regular meetings
of students and post-doc

00:20:24.810 --> 00:20:27.680
presenting their ideas to the
audience at large-- an audience

00:20:27.680 --> 00:20:29.680
that involves people from
different disciplines.

00:20:29.680 --> 00:20:31.782
And each time they
learn something new.

00:20:31.782 --> 00:20:33.990
And each time they walk away
thinking about something

00:20:33.990 --> 00:20:35.310
differently.

00:20:35.310 --> 00:20:37.980
This is part of the success.

00:20:37.980 --> 00:20:41.140
And they also teach each
other in the process.

00:20:41.140 --> 00:20:43.020
These are two
examples of programs

00:20:43.020 --> 00:20:45.890
run by students and
post-docs in the labs,

00:20:45.890 --> 00:20:48.130
again teaching each other
about the problems they

00:20:48.130 --> 00:20:51.000
know and the others
might not so well.

00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:53.020
And so this kind of
environment, this culture

00:20:53.020 --> 00:20:55.020
that we've created I think
is part of the reason

00:20:55.020 --> 00:20:56.760
why the Koch has worked.

00:20:56.760 --> 00:21:00.380
And I'm extremely proud and
grateful to all the people

00:21:00.380 --> 00:21:01.710
who've contributed.

00:21:01.710 --> 00:21:02.210
All right.

00:21:02.210 --> 00:21:03.860
So that's the Koch introduction.

00:21:03.860 --> 00:21:06.662
We're now going to switch
gears and talk about science.

00:21:06.662 --> 00:21:08.995
And because we're going to
switch gears and it's at MIT,

00:21:08.995 --> 00:21:12.120
it's appropriate that we
start with a test question.

00:21:12.120 --> 00:21:13.390
So here we are.

00:21:13.390 --> 00:21:17.110
Which of the following diseases
claimed the most lives in 2015?

00:21:17.110 --> 00:21:20.780
Is it A, HIV/AIDS, B,
tuberculosis, C, malaria,

00:21:20.780 --> 00:21:22.110
or D, cancer?

00:21:22.110 --> 00:21:24.090
Who thinks A?

00:21:24.090 --> 00:21:25.515
B?

00:21:25.515 --> 00:21:27.890
C?

00:21:27.890 --> 00:21:29.850
D?

00:21:29.850 --> 00:21:31.020
Libby, what's the answer?

00:21:31.020 --> 00:21:32.280
D, cancer.

00:21:32.280 --> 00:21:35.456
Of course it's cancer.

00:21:35.456 --> 00:21:38.840
This is a cancer lecture
after all, for god's sake.

00:21:38.840 --> 00:21:42.910
Cancer claims more lives in
the world than the other three

00:21:42.910 --> 00:21:43.900
combined.

00:21:43.900 --> 00:21:45.400
You might find that
hard to believe.

00:21:45.400 --> 00:21:48.850
But about 8.2 million people
lost their lives worldwide

00:21:48.850 --> 00:21:49.370
to cancer.

00:21:49.370 --> 00:21:51.320
That number is actually
going up sadly.

00:21:51.320 --> 00:21:53.130
So cancer is a big problem.

00:21:53.130 --> 00:21:54.580
It's the kind of
problem that MIT

00:21:54.580 --> 00:21:58.445
should be investing in time and
energy and resources to solve.

00:22:02.262 --> 00:22:04.220
So I need to give you a
little bit of a primer.

00:22:04.220 --> 00:22:07.810
This is an audience
that is diverse,

00:22:07.810 --> 00:22:09.000
coming from different areas.

00:22:09.000 --> 00:22:11.107
So I want to start
with the basics

00:22:11.107 --> 00:22:12.940
and then we'll move
ahead to talk about some

00:22:12.940 --> 00:22:15.380
of the cancer research that
we've pursued in our lab.

00:22:15.380 --> 00:22:18.130
So cancer cells arrive
from normal cells

00:22:18.130 --> 00:22:21.620
and acquire changes that allow
them to divide inappropriately.

00:22:21.620 --> 00:22:22.680
That's what cancer is.

00:22:22.680 --> 00:22:26.230
It's an inappropriate
accumulation of cells.

00:22:26.230 --> 00:22:30.100
Too many cells in the blood, too
many cells in a tissue, a mass.

00:22:30.100 --> 00:22:32.970
This process of uncontrolled
cell division and cell

00:22:32.970 --> 00:22:36.830
accumulation is driven by
changes in genes-- changes

00:22:36.830 --> 00:22:39.840
in the genes that control
the normal behavior of cells.

00:22:39.840 --> 00:22:41.480
And there are two
classes of genes

00:22:41.480 --> 00:22:44.350
that are classically involved
in cancer development--

00:22:44.350 --> 00:22:47.780
oncogenes which drive
the process forward.

00:22:47.780 --> 00:22:51.062
These genes become activated--
hyper-activated in cancer.

00:22:51.062 --> 00:22:52.770
And then there are
tumor-suppressor genes

00:22:52.770 --> 00:22:55.190
that normally act to
restrain this process.

00:22:55.190 --> 00:22:57.310
They get inactivated in cancer.

00:22:57.310 --> 00:22:58.640
They get eliminated.

00:22:58.640 --> 00:23:02.110
So these two classes of genes
underlie the development

00:23:02.110 --> 00:23:03.800
of all cancers.

00:23:03.800 --> 00:23:06.500
This is the genetic
basis of the disease.

00:23:06.500 --> 00:23:09.690
In addition, cancer cells
don't grow up in isolation.

00:23:09.690 --> 00:23:12.080
They grow up inside
tissues in your body.

00:23:12.080 --> 00:23:14.320
And as such they
interact with the quote,

00:23:14.320 --> 00:23:16.240
normal cells of your body too.

00:23:16.240 --> 00:23:19.870
And these interactions turn
out to be extremely important.

00:23:19.870 --> 00:23:23.640
They help the incipient
cancer cell evolve.

00:23:23.640 --> 00:23:25.560
And in some cases
they try to inhibit

00:23:25.560 --> 00:23:27.860
the development of those cells.

00:23:27.860 --> 00:23:29.940
Immune cells, for
example, can both promote

00:23:29.940 --> 00:23:33.630
and try to eliminate cancer
cells during their evolution.

00:23:33.630 --> 00:23:36.620
So cancer is a complex
process-- the inner workings

00:23:36.620 --> 00:23:39.160
of the cancer cell, as
well as the interactions

00:23:39.160 --> 00:23:42.510
between the cancer cell and
the cells that surround it.

00:23:42.510 --> 00:23:44.670
In addition, cancer
cells typically

00:23:44.670 --> 00:23:46.440
don't stay where they start.

00:23:46.440 --> 00:23:49.260
They move in the
process of metastasis

00:23:49.260 --> 00:23:51.510
to other sites in the body.

00:23:51.510 --> 00:23:54.130
And metastasis is actually
the lethal phase of cancer.

00:23:54.130 --> 00:23:56.630
About 90% of cancer
deaths are attributable

00:23:56.630 --> 00:23:58.506
to the metastatic
phase of the disease.

00:23:58.506 --> 00:24:00.880
It's a phase that we don't
actually understand very well.

00:24:00.880 --> 00:24:03.020
And we at MIT are actually
devoting a lot of energy

00:24:03.020 --> 00:24:05.235
to understand that
process better.

00:24:05.235 --> 00:24:07.360
I'm going to return to this
slide at several points

00:24:07.360 --> 00:24:10.360
in the lecture to show
you how we in the lab

00:24:10.360 --> 00:24:14.080
are approaching these
questions through the models

00:24:14.080 --> 00:24:16.330
that we've developed.

00:24:16.330 --> 00:24:18.570
A bit more background--
these genes

00:24:18.570 --> 00:24:21.220
that I mentioned lie
within your chromosomes.

00:24:21.220 --> 00:24:24.690
When you're born your cells
have the proper genetic dowry

00:24:24.690 --> 00:24:27.194
assembled on 23
pairs of chromosomes.

00:24:27.194 --> 00:24:29.360
Those chromosomes are painted
different colors here.

00:24:29.360 --> 00:24:32.390
This is what a normal
karyotype looks like.

00:24:32.390 --> 00:24:36.350
Cancer cells have
problems with their DNA.

00:24:36.350 --> 00:24:38.400
Here's an example
of a cancer cell

00:24:38.400 --> 00:24:40.130
which has way too
many chromosomes.

00:24:40.130 --> 00:24:41.500
You can appreciate that.

00:24:41.500 --> 00:24:42.770
This is called aneuploidy.

00:24:42.770 --> 00:24:44.950
Angelika Amon in
our institute is

00:24:44.950 --> 00:24:46.500
studying the details
of aneuploidy

00:24:46.500 --> 00:24:48.520
and how it
contributes to cancer.

00:24:48.520 --> 00:24:51.950
You can also see certain
structural changes in the DNA,

00:24:51.950 --> 00:24:54.130
like this chromosome
here which is made up

00:24:54.130 --> 00:24:55.130
of two different colors.

00:24:55.130 --> 00:24:56.780
This is what's called
a translocation, where

00:24:56.780 --> 00:24:59.363
one piece of one chromosome has
been stuck to a different one.

00:24:59.363 --> 00:25:02.470
This creates a new
gene, likely an oncogene

00:25:02.470 --> 00:25:05.210
which is driving these
cancer cells forward.

00:25:05.210 --> 00:25:08.460
Here's another example of a
chromosomal spread of a cancer

00:25:08.460 --> 00:25:08.960
cell.

00:25:08.960 --> 00:25:11.376
And you can see that these
chromosomes are all screwed up.

00:25:11.376 --> 00:25:12.570
There's multiple colors.

00:25:12.570 --> 00:25:14.659
This is genetic haywire.

00:25:14.659 --> 00:25:16.450
It's amazing that cancer
cells can actually

00:25:16.450 --> 00:25:19.570
live with all this chromosomal
damage and yet they do.

00:25:19.570 --> 00:25:23.220
They grow and ultimately
caused severe damage.

00:25:23.220 --> 00:25:25.450
Now within all of
those changes, we now

00:25:25.450 --> 00:25:27.280
know from massive
sequencing efforts

00:25:27.280 --> 00:25:31.090
of the genomes of thousands of
cancer specimens-- much of that

00:25:31.090 --> 00:25:33.040
done at the Broad
Institute-- that there

00:25:33.040 --> 00:25:36.660
are about 600 genes
that are mutated

00:25:36.660 --> 00:25:40.250
at significant frequency
across all human cancer.

00:25:40.250 --> 00:25:44.910
So 600 of the about 22,000
genes that are in your genome

00:25:44.910 --> 00:25:48.330
are directly contributing to
one or another form of cancer.

00:25:48.330 --> 00:25:49.692
That's a pretty large number.

00:25:49.692 --> 00:25:52.150
And that actually represents
some of the difficulties, some

00:25:52.150 --> 00:25:55.200
of the challenge that we
cancer researchers face,

00:25:55.200 --> 00:25:57.730
is to figure out the
contributions of all

00:25:57.730 --> 00:25:59.340
of those different
genes in order

00:25:59.340 --> 00:26:01.110
to be able to
understand the process

00:26:01.110 --> 00:26:03.010
and hopefully do
something about it.

00:26:03.010 --> 00:26:05.370
One of the promises
of figuring that out,

00:26:05.370 --> 00:26:08.326
once you understand the
molecular basis of cancer,

00:26:08.326 --> 00:26:10.200
is that you might be
able to treat it better.

00:26:10.200 --> 00:26:13.540
If you can figure out exactly
what's driving a cancer,

00:26:13.540 --> 00:26:15.410
you might be able to
make a medicine that

00:26:15.410 --> 00:26:18.750
overcomes that effect, that
directly inhibits that effect.

00:26:18.750 --> 00:26:21.580
This is the so-called idea
of precision cancer medicine.

00:26:21.580 --> 00:26:24.440
And we're in era of precision
cancer medicine today.

00:26:24.440 --> 00:26:26.910
There are new drugs
that can counteract

00:26:26.910 --> 00:26:29.000
the effects of
particular changes

00:26:29.000 --> 00:26:31.820
in certain genes
in certain cancers.

00:26:31.820 --> 00:26:34.590
Importantly, among the earliest
of these precision cancer

00:26:34.590 --> 00:26:38.420
medicines, two were based
on discoveries made at MIT.

00:26:38.420 --> 00:26:41.460
HER2, a gene discovered
in Bob Weinberg's lab

00:26:41.460 --> 00:26:44.820
is the target of a breast
cancer drug called Herceptin.

00:26:44.820 --> 00:26:47.130
It works extremely
well in women who have

00:26:47.130 --> 00:26:49.260
that particular alteration.

00:26:49.260 --> 00:26:51.650
It's useless for
women who don't.

00:26:51.650 --> 00:26:53.840
BCR-ABL is a small
molecule drug that

00:26:53.840 --> 00:26:56.170
inhibits the function
of an enzyme that's

00:26:56.170 --> 00:26:59.350
overactive in certain leukemias.

00:26:59.350 --> 00:27:02.230
The enzyme ABL was discovered
in David Baltimore's lab here

00:27:02.230 --> 00:27:03.610
at MIT.

00:27:03.610 --> 00:27:06.770
Crizotinib treats a certain
form of lung cancer based

00:27:06.770 --> 00:27:08.419
on a particular alteration.

00:27:08.419 --> 00:27:09.835
I'm particularly
proud of that one

00:27:09.835 --> 00:27:11.930
because the
international study that

00:27:11.930 --> 00:27:16.670
was done to lead to the
approval of this drug

00:27:16.670 --> 00:27:18.530
was carried out
by Alice Shaw, who

00:27:18.530 --> 00:27:20.280
used to be a post-doc
in my lab and is now

00:27:20.280 --> 00:27:23.100
an independent investigator
at the Mass General Hospital.

00:27:23.100 --> 00:27:24.830
So we're in an era
now where we've

00:27:24.830 --> 00:27:26.880
translated this basic
understanding of cancer

00:27:26.880 --> 00:27:31.190
genetics to some, but not
yet enough new medicines that

00:27:31.190 --> 00:27:33.370
are more effective
and less toxic

00:27:33.370 --> 00:27:35.390
than standard chemotherapy.

00:27:35.390 --> 00:27:39.150
Now this process of cancer
genes and cancer genetics

00:27:39.150 --> 00:27:40.260
has a long history.

00:27:40.260 --> 00:27:42.700
Our understanding goes
back many, many years.

00:27:42.700 --> 00:27:46.300
It actually goes back
to this institution.

00:27:46.300 --> 00:27:50.260
The first cancer gene was
discovered by Bob Weinberg.

00:27:50.260 --> 00:27:55.590
He found it in a study
published here with Chiaho Shih

00:27:55.590 --> 00:27:59.010
while Bob's lab was still
in the cancer center.

00:27:59.010 --> 00:28:01.730
This was the first demonstration
that cancer cells, human cancer

00:28:01.730 --> 00:28:04.820
cells have altered genes.

00:28:04.820 --> 00:28:07.490
This really set the
field in motion,

00:28:07.490 --> 00:28:10.760
and frankly set my
career in motion.

00:28:10.760 --> 00:28:14.520
I was an undergraduate when
this study was published.

00:28:14.520 --> 00:28:17.190
And Bob came and gave
a lecture and a course

00:28:17.190 --> 00:28:20.070
that I attended and
described this work.

00:28:20.070 --> 00:28:22.820
And it was based
on that work that I

00:28:22.820 --> 00:28:24.830
decided I would become
a cancer researcher.

00:28:24.830 --> 00:28:26.190
So I owe it all to you, Bob.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:32.990
A few years later, Bob's
lab cloned the first tumor

00:28:32.990 --> 00:28:35.530
suppressor gene in collaboration
with Ted [INAUDIBLE] lab.

00:28:35.530 --> 00:28:37.580
This is the retinoblastoma
tumor suppressor gene.

00:28:37.580 --> 00:28:39.038
A few years later
David [INAUDIBLE]

00:28:39.038 --> 00:28:43.880
lab cloned the second or third
tumor suppressor gene, WT-1.

00:28:43.880 --> 00:28:46.150
So MIT researchers have
contributed significantly

00:28:46.150 --> 00:28:49.310
to the discovery of cancer
genes over the years.

00:28:49.310 --> 00:28:53.160
The retinoblastoma gene falls as
a good example of a gene that's

00:28:53.160 --> 00:28:57.990
mutated not only in sporadically
occurring cancers, where

00:28:57.990 --> 00:29:00.682
there's no family history of
the disease-- this gene is

00:29:00.682 --> 00:29:02.640
responsible for the
development of a rare tumor

00:29:02.640 --> 00:29:04.960
of the eye, retinoblastoma.

00:29:04.960 --> 00:29:06.750
But also you can
inherit from one parent

00:29:06.750 --> 00:29:09.140
a defective copy of
this gene, and thereby

00:29:09.140 --> 00:29:12.490
have a familial predisposition
to retinoblastoma.

00:29:12.490 --> 00:29:14.760
And that disease looks
something like this.

00:29:14.760 --> 00:29:17.980
This is a child with
so-called bilateral multifocal

00:29:17.980 --> 00:29:19.360
retinoblastoma.

00:29:19.360 --> 00:29:22.415
This child has tumors in both
eyes, probably about a dozen

00:29:22.415 --> 00:29:23.205
in total.

00:29:23.205 --> 00:29:24.955
And this is due to the
fact that the child

00:29:24.955 --> 00:29:27.760
has inherited one defective
copy of the retinoblastoma tumor

00:29:27.760 --> 00:29:30.130
suppressor gene from one
parent, and is therefore just

00:29:30.130 --> 00:29:33.150
one mutational event away from
lacking this gene altogether.

00:29:33.150 --> 00:29:36.730
And when that happens
the tumors develop.

00:29:36.730 --> 00:29:39.329
When this work was
done in Bob's lab,

00:29:39.329 --> 00:29:41.245
I decided that this is
what I wanted to study.

00:29:41.245 --> 00:29:43.520
And I decided actually
to come back to Boston,

00:29:43.520 --> 00:29:47.680
come back to MIT and join
Bob's lab to study this disease

00:29:47.680 --> 00:29:50.080
and particularly familial
predisposition to cancer--

00:29:50.080 --> 00:29:52.890
the genetic basis of cancer.

00:29:52.890 --> 00:29:55.220
And I used techniques
that had been developed

00:29:55.220 --> 00:29:57.470
by other people in
the field to allow

00:29:57.470 --> 00:29:59.884
us to manipulate the genes
of mice in particular.

00:29:59.884 --> 00:30:02.050
We can do this in other
species now, but at the time

00:30:02.050 --> 00:30:03.670
we could really
only do it in mice.

00:30:03.670 --> 00:30:06.040
I won't go through the
details of these studies,

00:30:06.040 --> 00:30:08.050
but suffice it to say
that we can manipulate

00:30:08.050 --> 00:30:11.960
the genomes of mice by adding
extra genes, oncogenes,

00:30:11.960 --> 00:30:14.820
or deleting certain genes,
tumor-suppressor genes,

00:30:14.820 --> 00:30:17.620
and by virtue of that make
those mice cancer-prone.

00:30:17.620 --> 00:30:20.450
And that's what these two
studies published by other labs

00:30:20.450 --> 00:30:21.510
demonstrate.

00:30:21.510 --> 00:30:23.990
So I decided that I
would use the techniques

00:30:23.990 --> 00:30:27.510
of genetic engineering in
mice to make models of cancer

00:30:27.510 --> 00:30:30.800
that we could then use to study
the disease process in ways

00:30:30.800 --> 00:30:33.380
that are difficult to
do in human beings.

00:30:33.380 --> 00:30:36.000
So the idea was to create
genetically engineered mice

00:30:36.000 --> 00:30:39.240
with mutations in
cancer genes, create

00:30:39.240 --> 00:30:41.030
models of various
types of cancer,

00:30:41.030 --> 00:30:42.680
and then study those models.

00:30:42.680 --> 00:30:45.230
And over the years we've
studied many different cancer

00:30:45.230 --> 00:30:47.440
types-- lung cancer,
which is going to be

00:30:47.440 --> 00:30:48.950
a theme of today's lecture.

00:30:48.950 --> 00:30:52.120
We also study pancreas cancer,
sarcomas, ovarian cancer,

00:30:52.120 --> 00:30:56.320
brain cancer, retinoblastoma I
mentioned, and several others.

00:30:56.320 --> 00:30:58.520
Now once you have
these strains of mice

00:30:58.520 --> 00:31:01.260
that are genetically
engineered to be cancer-prone

00:31:01.260 --> 00:31:04.386
you can use them for various
things, including for example,

00:31:04.386 --> 00:31:05.510
studying tumor development.

00:31:05.510 --> 00:31:07.460
And that was really the main
purpose at the beginning.

00:31:07.460 --> 00:31:09.626
We don't understand the
process of tumor development

00:31:09.626 --> 00:31:10.250
well enough.

00:31:10.250 --> 00:31:13.261
It's hard to study that
process in a human being.

00:31:13.261 --> 00:31:14.760
But with a mouse
model you can do it

00:31:14.760 --> 00:31:17.580
because you can access the
disease at various stages.

00:31:17.580 --> 00:31:19.330
You can look at the
diseased cells in ways

00:31:19.330 --> 00:31:21.720
you can't easily do in
people and so forth.

00:31:21.720 --> 00:31:24.370
So you can understand what
we call tumor progression

00:31:24.370 --> 00:31:27.460
through the analysis
of these mouse models.

00:31:27.460 --> 00:31:29.550
In addition, once
you have them, they

00:31:29.550 --> 00:31:31.350
can be very useful practically.

00:31:31.350 --> 00:31:34.420
You can use these models to
understand early detection.

00:31:34.420 --> 00:31:36.792
Maybe I can find the
disease in an earlier stage

00:31:36.792 --> 00:31:38.250
then it becomes
clinically evident.

00:31:38.250 --> 00:31:39.416
That would be highly useful.

00:31:39.416 --> 00:31:41.439
And we'll return to that topic.

00:31:41.439 --> 00:31:42.980
Maybe we can figure
out new therapies

00:31:42.980 --> 00:31:44.563
to treat the disease
more effectively.

00:31:44.563 --> 00:31:47.240
If I understand the process
better by these mouse models,

00:31:47.240 --> 00:31:49.690
maybe I can use a new
therapy to block it, and then

00:31:49.690 --> 00:31:52.300
use that new therapy in
humans more effectively.

00:31:52.300 --> 00:31:54.420
Or maybe I can prevent
the disease altogether.

00:31:54.420 --> 00:31:57.200
If I understand the process
really, really well,

00:31:57.200 --> 00:31:59.310
and be able to
anticipate it, maybe I

00:31:59.310 --> 00:32:01.350
can actually intervene
before it gets

00:32:01.350 --> 00:32:04.650
to an advanced stage in the
form of cancer prevention.

00:32:04.650 --> 00:32:07.617
And we'll turn to
those topics as well.

00:32:07.617 --> 00:32:09.450
So the first mouse I
made-- which by the way

00:32:09.450 --> 00:32:13.110
took four years of my
life, back-breaking effort,

00:32:13.110 --> 00:32:13.860
Bob will remember.

00:32:13.860 --> 00:32:14.660
He's smiling.

00:32:14.660 --> 00:32:18.120
Thanks a lot for that.

00:32:18.120 --> 00:32:19.990
Nothing worked for
me in Bob's lab.

00:32:19.990 --> 00:32:20.960
I blame him.

00:32:20.960 --> 00:32:22.640
Nothing worked for me.

00:32:22.640 --> 00:32:24.530
But we struggled on
and we made this mouse

00:32:24.530 --> 00:32:26.920
that had a germ line mutation
in the retinoblastoma gene

00:32:26.920 --> 00:32:29.860
with a full expectation that
the animals would develop

00:32:29.860 --> 00:32:31.440
retinoblastoma, a tumor of eye.

00:32:31.440 --> 00:32:34.900
In fact they did develop
cancer, pituitary tumors

00:32:34.900 --> 00:32:39.610
at 100% frequency, thyroid
tumors at 50% frequency.

00:32:39.610 --> 00:32:42.260
But amazingly enough,
not a single one of them

00:32:42.260 --> 00:32:44.030
developed retinoblastoma.

00:32:44.030 --> 00:32:48.010
And frankly speaking,
that was frustrating.

00:32:48.010 --> 00:32:50.690
Now we went on, over
the next several years

00:32:50.690 --> 00:32:53.460
to figure out why it was
that mice with a germ line

00:32:53.460 --> 00:32:56.630
mutation in retinoblastoma, the
genetic analogs of that child

00:32:56.630 --> 00:32:58.350
that I showed you
a few minutes ago,

00:32:58.350 --> 00:33:00.490
actually don't develop
retinoblastoma.

00:33:00.490 --> 00:33:04.760
And the answer is because there
are two other retinoblastoma

00:33:04.760 --> 00:33:08.800
related genes that go by the
imaginative names of p107

00:33:08.800 --> 00:33:10.800
and p130.

00:33:10.800 --> 00:33:12.990
These are structurally
similar to Rb

00:33:12.990 --> 00:33:14.690
and functionally overlapping.

00:33:14.690 --> 00:33:18.135
Some of that work's shown by
Jackie Lee's here at the Koch.

00:33:18.135 --> 00:33:19.510
And it turns out
that in a mouse,

00:33:19.510 --> 00:33:21.710
for some reason in
the mouse, they can

00:33:21.710 --> 00:33:24.010
cover for loss of Rb function.

00:33:24.010 --> 00:33:27.030
So it turns out that you have
to get rid of 107 or 130,

00:33:27.030 --> 00:33:29.480
in combination with
Rb, and when you

00:33:29.480 --> 00:33:33.330
do that, finally in the hands
of David MacPherson in the lab,

00:33:33.330 --> 00:33:34.610
you get retinoblastomas.

00:33:34.610 --> 00:33:36.740
It took 15 years
for us to get there

00:33:36.740 --> 00:33:38.610
but we eventually succeeded.

00:33:38.610 --> 00:33:41.990
And so by sort of
understanding the underlying

00:33:41.990 --> 00:33:43.620
biology and the
underlying genetics,

00:33:43.620 --> 00:33:46.020
we could improve these
models over time.

00:33:46.020 --> 00:33:49.820
And that's been an
ongoing theme for us.

00:33:49.820 --> 00:33:52.040
A major focus since I
moved to the cancer center

00:33:52.040 --> 00:33:54.520
and then on to the Koch
has been lung cancer.

00:33:54.520 --> 00:33:56.740
Lung cancer is a major
cancer type in the US.

00:33:56.740 --> 00:33:59.550
It's responsible for about
160 cancer deaths in the US,

00:33:59.550 --> 00:34:01.760
more than a million worldwide.

00:34:01.760 --> 00:34:04.920
Of course smoking is a major
carcinogen for lung cancer,

00:34:04.920 --> 00:34:08.500
responsible for about
90% of cancer deaths.

00:34:08.500 --> 00:34:12.804
There are various types of lung
cancer, small cell lung cancer,

00:34:12.804 --> 00:34:13.929
non-small cell lung cancer.

00:34:13.929 --> 00:34:15.690
You might have
heard these terms.

00:34:15.690 --> 00:34:17.500
The type that we
studied most extensively

00:34:17.500 --> 00:34:18.967
is called adenocarcinoma.

00:34:18.967 --> 00:34:20.800
It's one of the non-small
cell lung cancers.

00:34:20.800 --> 00:34:24.040
It happens to be the most
frequent of the lung cancers.

00:34:24.040 --> 00:34:27.360
And we know a bit about
the genetics that underlie

00:34:27.360 --> 00:34:28.480
this form of lung cancer.

00:34:28.480 --> 00:34:30.889
We know, based on genome
sequencing analysis,

00:34:30.889 --> 00:34:34.130
for example, that two genes
are frequently mutated.

00:34:34.130 --> 00:34:36.570
K-ras, which is one of
these oncogenes that

00:34:36.570 --> 00:34:40.389
drives the process forward, and
p53, which is one of the tumor

00:34:40.389 --> 00:34:43.540
suppressor genes that
normally blocks the process

00:34:43.540 --> 00:34:46.659
and is inactivated in cancers.

00:34:46.659 --> 00:34:49.659
I also mentioned a couple of
other genes here, EGFR and ALK.

00:34:49.659 --> 00:34:52.550
These occur at lower
frequency in lung cancers.

00:34:52.550 --> 00:34:54.993
I mention them because
they are the target

00:34:54.993 --> 00:34:57.159
of some of these targeted
therapies for lung cancer.

00:34:57.159 --> 00:35:00.980
There are drugs that treat
these hyperactive genes.

00:35:00.980 --> 00:35:03.720
But based on the frequent
mutation of K-ras and p53

00:35:03.720 --> 00:35:06.850
we decided that we would study
those genes in the development

00:35:06.850 --> 00:35:07.710
of lung cancer.

00:35:07.710 --> 00:35:09.880
And we made a mouse
that we study now very

00:35:09.880 --> 00:35:10.900
extensively in my lab.

00:35:10.900 --> 00:35:13.191
There's a little bit of
scientific detail on the slide.

00:35:13.191 --> 00:35:14.499
It doesn't really matter.

00:35:14.499 --> 00:35:16.290
Suffice it to say that
we made a mouse that

00:35:16.290 --> 00:35:18.820
has genetically engineered
copies of the K-ras

00:35:18.820 --> 00:35:21.820
oncogene and the p53
tumor suppressor gene.

00:35:21.820 --> 00:35:24.210
But in the native state
those don't do anything

00:35:24.210 --> 00:35:25.970
to the cells of these
animals, including

00:35:25.970 --> 00:35:27.320
the cells of the lung.

00:35:27.320 --> 00:35:31.760
However they're triggered to be
activated in the case of K-ras

00:35:31.760 --> 00:35:34.660
and inactivated in
the case of p-53.

00:35:34.660 --> 00:35:37.600
And the trigger occurs by virtue
of these little triangular

00:35:37.600 --> 00:35:38.280
shapes here.

00:35:38.280 --> 00:35:39.980
These are called loxP sites.

00:35:39.980 --> 00:35:42.580
And when this enzyme
called Cre acts

00:35:42.580 --> 00:35:45.630
to loop out those
sequences, this K-ras gene

00:35:45.630 --> 00:35:49.600
goes from off to on, and the
p53 tumor suppressor gene

00:35:49.600 --> 00:35:51.500
goes from on to off.

00:35:51.500 --> 00:35:54.100
And that's enough to
initiate a cancer.

00:35:54.100 --> 00:35:56.140
And therefore over
time in these animals

00:35:56.140 --> 00:35:59.850
we get lung tumors in the
lung, and over further time

00:35:59.850 --> 00:36:02.610
we see these tumors
progress to metastasize.

00:36:02.610 --> 00:36:05.340
And so this is a model that
we've now studied extensively

00:36:05.340 --> 00:36:09.350
to understand the various steps
of lung cancer development.

00:36:09.350 --> 00:36:13.290
We've studied this many ways,
by investigators, some of whom

00:36:13.290 --> 00:36:15.600
are in the room,
over many years.

00:36:15.600 --> 00:36:17.470
This is the contributions
of the K-ras

00:36:17.470 --> 00:36:20.450
oncogene to the early
stages of tumor development.

00:36:20.450 --> 00:36:22.870
This is actually a baby
picture of a tumor,

00:36:22.870 --> 00:36:25.810
contributed by Carla Kim,
sitting right over there.

00:36:25.810 --> 00:36:28.260
This is the very earliest
steps of response

00:36:28.260 --> 00:36:30.850
to K-ras activation--
hyper-proliferation

00:36:30.850 --> 00:36:32.040
of those cells.

00:36:32.040 --> 00:36:35.670
They form these little layers of
abnormally over-growing cells,

00:36:35.670 --> 00:36:38.230
benign tumors called
adenomas that progress

00:36:38.230 --> 00:36:40.130
to larger adenomas.

00:36:40.130 --> 00:36:42.840
We now know that if you
inactivate the p53 tumor

00:36:42.840 --> 00:36:44.710
suppressor gene,
those tumors will

00:36:44.710 --> 00:36:48.550
progress from a benign
state to a malignant state.

00:36:48.550 --> 00:36:51.280
They go from benign
to real cancer.

00:36:51.280 --> 00:36:53.770
p53 controls that transition.

00:36:53.770 --> 00:36:55.870
And over the years
others have contributed

00:36:55.870 --> 00:36:58.040
our understanding of many
of these various steps,

00:36:58.040 --> 00:36:59.130
these arrows.

00:36:59.130 --> 00:37:01.650
We're developing sort of
a molecular cartography

00:37:01.650 --> 00:37:03.610
of cancer development
here, filling

00:37:03.610 --> 00:37:05.140
in the pieces of this puzzle.

00:37:05.140 --> 00:37:07.940
And we now understand
it actually quite well.

00:37:07.940 --> 00:37:09.930
Now why do we
bother to understand

00:37:09.930 --> 00:37:14.440
what these steps are, these
various active pathways, genes

00:37:14.440 --> 00:37:16.182
that become hyper-expressed?

00:37:16.182 --> 00:37:17.890
And the reason is, as
I mentioned before,

00:37:17.890 --> 00:37:20.670
that if we can understand that
in detail, in the mouse model

00:37:20.670 --> 00:37:23.180
and ultimately in
people, we might

00:37:23.180 --> 00:37:26.660
be able to treat this
disease more effectively.

00:37:26.660 --> 00:37:28.080
Now I work on mouse models.

00:37:28.080 --> 00:37:30.130
And many people believe,
and I bet many of you

00:37:30.130 --> 00:37:33.730
realize that there's a long
history of slamming mouse

00:37:33.730 --> 00:37:35.390
models of cancer.

00:37:35.390 --> 00:37:37.770
Many people think that it's
really easy to cure cancer

00:37:37.770 --> 00:37:40.374
in mice, and therefore
it's not really worth

00:37:40.374 --> 00:37:42.040
doing the experiment
in mice if it's not

00:37:42.040 --> 00:37:45.010
going to prove true in humans.

00:37:45.010 --> 00:37:46.690
In fact, those
statements are based

00:37:46.690 --> 00:37:50.470
on primitive models, primitive
mouse models of cancer.

00:37:50.470 --> 00:37:53.200
These models are much more
faithful to the actual disease.

00:37:53.200 --> 00:37:55.140
They look much
more similar, both

00:37:55.140 --> 00:37:58.350
genetically and
histopathologically and related

00:37:58.350 --> 00:38:00.879
to the various cells that are
interacting with the tumor.

00:38:00.879 --> 00:38:02.420
And so for all those
people who think

00:38:02.420 --> 00:38:04.850
that it's really easy
to cure cancer in mice,

00:38:04.850 --> 00:38:06.709
I say cure this.

00:38:06.709 --> 00:38:08.750
And I've actually put my
money where my mouth is.

00:38:08.750 --> 00:38:13.580
I've offered $10,000 to anybody
who can cure this model.

00:38:13.580 --> 00:38:16.740
I never offer my own money.

00:38:16.740 --> 00:38:19.710
Today I will offer
President Rief's money.

00:38:19.710 --> 00:38:21.550
[LAUGHTER]

00:38:22.930 --> 00:38:28.000
He's tapping David Koch on the
shoulder, I'll have you know.

00:38:28.000 --> 00:38:30.770
Many have tried, no one
has succeeded to date.

00:38:30.770 --> 00:38:31.870
It's very difficult to do.

00:38:31.870 --> 00:38:34.070
Cancer is a tough challenge.

00:38:34.070 --> 00:38:35.020
We've tried as well.

00:38:35.020 --> 00:38:37.650
Here's two quick
examples-- inhibitors

00:38:37.650 --> 00:38:39.150
against one of those
pathways called

00:38:39.150 --> 00:38:41.140
NF-kappa B, inhibitors
against another

00:38:41.140 --> 00:38:44.340
of those pathways I mentioned
that gets hyperactivated, went.

00:38:44.340 --> 00:38:47.530
These extend lifespan of
mice as you can see here.

00:38:47.530 --> 00:38:50.230
Animals that are untreated
succumb to their disease,

00:38:50.230 --> 00:38:52.460
in this case within
about 100 days.

00:38:52.460 --> 00:38:54.770
If you treat with
this small molecule

00:38:54.770 --> 00:38:57.930
drug or this small molecule drug
the animals live a bit longer.

00:38:57.930 --> 00:38:59.740
This is clearly not
curative but it's

00:38:59.740 --> 00:39:01.169
a step in the right direction.

00:39:01.169 --> 00:39:02.960
And we think that these
treatments, perhaps

00:39:02.960 --> 00:39:04.210
in combination with
other treatments

00:39:04.210 --> 00:39:06.543
might actually get us to
something that could be useful.

00:39:06.543 --> 00:39:08.810
And we're encouraging our
pharmaceutical partners

00:39:08.810 --> 00:39:12.280
to pursue these drugs actually
for the study of treatment

00:39:12.280 --> 00:39:13.620
in humans.

00:39:13.620 --> 00:39:15.250
These two drugs
fall into a class.

00:39:15.250 --> 00:39:17.310
We call them small
molecule inhibitors.

00:39:17.310 --> 00:39:19.640
They're useful in
binding to enzymes

00:39:19.640 --> 00:39:22.990
that get hyperactive in cancer
cells in turning them off.

00:39:22.990 --> 00:39:24.990
They fit like a lock in a key.

00:39:24.990 --> 00:39:26.620
There's another class
of cancer drugs,

00:39:26.620 --> 00:39:28.320
the antibody-based drugs.

00:39:28.320 --> 00:39:30.740
These are much larger
biological molecules.

00:39:30.740 --> 00:39:32.630
Herceptin is an example.

00:39:32.630 --> 00:39:34.460
They can bind to
proteins on the outside

00:39:34.460 --> 00:39:38.390
of cells that get hyperactivated
and turn them off.

00:39:38.390 --> 00:39:41.407
These are good and where they
can be used can be powerful,

00:39:41.407 --> 00:39:42.490
but they're quite limited.

00:39:42.490 --> 00:39:45.100
It's not always easy to find
an enzyme that's hyperactive

00:39:45.100 --> 00:39:46.760
in cancer cells in
order to drug it,

00:39:46.760 --> 00:39:49.150
or to find a protein on the
surface of cells you could

00:39:49.150 --> 00:39:50.750
that you can inhibit
by an antibody.

00:39:50.750 --> 00:39:52.330
It would be nice
if we had a tool

00:39:52.330 --> 00:39:54.710
that we could use to
turn off the expression

00:39:54.710 --> 00:39:57.880
of all genes that are
hyperactive in cancer cells.

00:39:57.880 --> 00:40:02.540
And it turns out, in part based
on discoveries from MIT alums,

00:40:02.540 --> 00:40:05.700
including folks trained by
Bob Horovitz and Phil Sharp,

00:40:05.700 --> 00:40:07.280
that there is a
new class of drugs

00:40:07.280 --> 00:40:12.360
emerging, so-called RNA-based
drugs-- small RNA molecules

00:40:12.360 --> 00:40:17.996
that can bind to and
inhibit target mRNAs

00:40:17.996 --> 00:40:19.490
and cause either
their functional

00:40:19.490 --> 00:40:23.420
silencing or their
activation, their elimination.

00:40:23.420 --> 00:40:27.010
And if you could get these
small RNAs into cancer cells,

00:40:27.010 --> 00:40:29.240
you might be able to
turn off an oncogene.

00:40:29.240 --> 00:40:32.330
There's another class
of RNAs called microRNAs

00:40:32.330 --> 00:40:36.410
which can silence pathways that
get hyperactivated when tumor

00:40:36.410 --> 00:40:37.990
suppressor genes are lost.

00:40:37.990 --> 00:40:40.530
If you could put a microRNA
inside a cancer cell

00:40:40.530 --> 00:40:42.850
that lacked a particular
tumor suppressor gene,

00:40:42.850 --> 00:40:45.260
you might be able to
correct the defect caused

00:40:45.260 --> 00:40:47.466
by the loss of that
tumor suppressor gene.

00:40:47.466 --> 00:40:49.090
And so we decided
some years ago to see

00:40:49.090 --> 00:40:51.260
if this could work,
in particular using

00:40:51.260 --> 00:40:54.685
these small interfering RNAs
to target ras in this model,

00:40:54.685 --> 00:40:58.320
or a microRNA that can
substitute at least in part

00:40:58.320 --> 00:41:00.480
for the loss of p53.

00:41:00.480 --> 00:41:03.690
Now the difficulty is that it's
hard to get these microRNAs

00:41:03.690 --> 00:41:05.330
into cancer cells in the body.

00:41:05.330 --> 00:41:08.602
If you stick a naked RNA into
the blood, it gets degraded.

00:41:08.602 --> 00:41:10.185
If you package it
up in certain things

00:41:10.185 --> 00:41:12.419
it mostly goes to the liver.

00:41:12.419 --> 00:41:13.960
It's fine for treating
liver disease,

00:41:13.960 --> 00:41:16.500
and Phil actually helped
start a company called Alnylam

00:41:16.500 --> 00:41:18.720
that is developing
liver-based treatments using

00:41:18.720 --> 00:41:20.397
this very strategy.

00:41:20.397 --> 00:41:22.980
But if you want to treat cancers
of the lung or somewhere else

00:41:22.980 --> 00:41:24.170
that's tricky.

00:41:24.170 --> 00:41:27.180
Now fortunately in the
Koch we have many folks

00:41:27.180 --> 00:41:29.350
who are developing
new strategies

00:41:29.350 --> 00:41:32.840
to deliver various
things, including

00:41:32.840 --> 00:41:36.890
these small RNAs to tumor
cells outside of the liver.

00:41:36.890 --> 00:41:39.710
And Dan Anderson's lab has
been one of the pioneers,

00:41:39.710 --> 00:41:41.250
along with Bob Langer's lab.

00:41:41.250 --> 00:41:43.910
And in Dan's lab,
James Dahlman developed

00:41:43.910 --> 00:41:46.380
a particular
nanoparticle that they

00:41:46.380 --> 00:41:49.640
showed was able to
carry these small RNAs,

00:41:49.640 --> 00:41:52.500
not just to the liver but
to other places, including,

00:41:52.500 --> 00:41:54.650
importantly here, the lung.

00:41:54.650 --> 00:41:58.300
So Wen Xue in my lab
collaborated with James Dahlman

00:41:58.300 --> 00:42:01.530
and tested the ability
of nanoparticles

00:42:01.530 --> 00:42:04.370
that were loaded
up with microRNA 34

00:42:04.370 --> 00:42:07.410
or si-RNAs RNAs against
K-ras, or the combination

00:42:07.410 --> 00:42:11.510
to treat effectively the lung
cancers in this model system.

00:42:11.510 --> 00:42:14.390
And sure enough, when
we treat these animals

00:42:14.390 --> 00:42:18.570
with multiple doses-- animals
caring establish tumors--

00:42:18.570 --> 00:42:21.050
in contrast to control tumors
that contribute continue

00:42:21.050 --> 00:42:26.880
to grow, the miR34 treated
tumors show growth inhibition.

00:42:26.880 --> 00:42:29.600
So this small RNA
therapy actually works.

00:42:29.600 --> 00:42:32.520
It slows the growth
of these tumors.

00:42:32.520 --> 00:42:36.360
Likewise if we inhibit K-ras
with this si-RNA against K-ras

00:42:36.360 --> 00:42:39.690
we can see a tumor
growth inhibition.

00:42:39.690 --> 00:42:42.080
If we combine those
two, we can actually

00:42:42.080 --> 00:42:46.490
see tumor regression taking
place-- an RNA-based treatment

00:42:46.490 --> 00:42:47.320
for cancer.

00:42:47.320 --> 00:42:49.620
This opens up a
whole new strategy

00:42:49.620 --> 00:42:52.600
for the treatment of this
and many other cancers.

00:42:52.600 --> 00:42:54.460
And with respect to
longevity as well,

00:42:54.460 --> 00:42:56.740
we can expand the lifespan,
as well as those drugs.

00:42:56.740 --> 00:42:59.959
In fact better than those drugs
that I showed you previously.

00:42:59.959 --> 00:43:02.500
Now you would think that this
would be an attractive strategy

00:43:02.500 --> 00:43:03.850
for pharmaceutical companies.

00:43:03.850 --> 00:43:06.210
We actually tried to attract
pharmaceutical companies

00:43:06.210 --> 00:43:08.890
to support this work
and translate this work.

00:43:08.890 --> 00:43:10.710
The scientists in that
particular company

00:43:10.710 --> 00:43:14.470
were really interested and
then the lawyers got involved.

00:43:14.470 --> 00:43:16.740
And sadly they killed
the project for reasons

00:43:16.740 --> 00:43:18.360
that I can explain later.

00:43:18.360 --> 00:43:20.840
But this I think is a
promising new approach

00:43:20.840 --> 00:43:22.730
to the treatment of
this and hopefully

00:43:22.730 --> 00:43:25.990
many other types of cancer.

00:43:25.990 --> 00:43:28.590
So I want to switch gears
and talk about this process

00:43:28.590 --> 00:43:30.730
of cancer genetics.

00:43:30.730 --> 00:43:33.610
Cancer genes as I mentioned
are a very important process

00:43:33.610 --> 00:43:36.080
in tumor development
most broadly.

00:43:36.080 --> 00:43:39.130
This subject has become
much more complex

00:43:39.130 --> 00:43:43.510
of late, in part because cancer
genome sequencing studies have

00:43:43.510 --> 00:43:46.170
revealed that many, many
genes-- I mentioned this

00:43:46.170 --> 00:43:48.670
before-- many, many
genes are mutated

00:43:48.670 --> 00:43:50.890
in various types
of human cancer.

00:43:50.890 --> 00:43:53.580
In lung cancer, for
example, the average tumor

00:43:53.580 --> 00:43:57.760
has about 175
protein-altering mutations.

00:43:57.760 --> 00:44:00.261
That's a bewildering
collection of changes.

00:44:00.261 --> 00:44:01.760
And it makes it
difficult for people

00:44:01.760 --> 00:44:04.630
like those in my lab to
think about how to study

00:44:04.630 --> 00:44:06.790
that process systematically.

00:44:06.790 --> 00:44:09.270
How do we look at all
of those different genes

00:44:09.270 --> 00:44:12.214
and their particular
contributions to cancer?

00:44:12.214 --> 00:44:13.880
And the reason it's
hard is that the way

00:44:13.880 --> 00:44:16.710
we do that or have
done that in the lab

00:44:16.710 --> 00:44:19.250
is to make mice with
mutations in those genes,

00:44:19.250 --> 00:44:21.360
and then to cross those
mice with the mice that

00:44:21.360 --> 00:44:23.910
are cancer-prone, and then
test the contributions

00:44:23.910 --> 00:44:27.160
of those mutations to
the tumorigenic process.

00:44:27.160 --> 00:44:30.450
That process would take two
to three years for every gene

00:44:30.450 --> 00:44:31.540
that you want to study.

00:44:31.540 --> 00:44:33.690
And that was bewildering to us.

00:44:33.690 --> 00:44:35.690
And frankly a bit discouraging.

00:44:35.690 --> 00:44:38.780
And around this time, as we
realized the daunting challenge

00:44:38.780 --> 00:44:43.380
that lay ahead of us, those
in the lab became depressed.

00:44:43.380 --> 00:44:46.420
The skies above
us were darkening.

00:44:46.420 --> 00:44:48.480
And we didn't know
quite what to do.

00:44:48.480 --> 00:44:50.070
So I called Ted Cruz.

00:44:50.070 --> 00:44:52.070
[LAUGHTER]

00:44:53.570 --> 00:44:57.790
And he said, "look to
the heavens and pray."

00:44:57.790 --> 00:44:58.750
So we did.

00:44:58.750 --> 00:45:02.850
And sure enough, the skies
brightened and delivered

00:45:02.850 --> 00:45:07.596
unto us was a tool,
and it's called CRISPR.

00:45:07.596 --> 00:45:10.060
Now I'll go through
this quite quickly.

00:45:10.060 --> 00:45:12.630
CRISPR is a remarkable
new tool available

00:45:12.630 --> 00:45:15.050
to biological scientists.

00:45:15.050 --> 00:45:17.600
It's based on a
system in bacteria

00:45:17.600 --> 00:45:21.840
that leads to the inactivation
of viruses that are trying

00:45:21.840 --> 00:45:25.370
to infect the bacterial cells.

00:45:25.370 --> 00:45:28.330
Various workers have
perfected this system

00:45:28.330 --> 00:45:31.530
and morphed it into a form
where it can be used effectively

00:45:31.530 --> 00:45:33.360
in mammalian cells.

00:45:33.360 --> 00:45:35.320
This work was carried
out by many labs,

00:45:35.320 --> 00:45:37.650
including Feng Zhang's
lab here at MIT,

00:45:37.650 --> 00:45:40.410
but important contributions by
Jennifer Doudna and Emmanuelle

00:45:40.410 --> 00:45:42.580
Charpentier, George
Church and others.

00:45:42.580 --> 00:45:44.290
We now have this
tool which allows

00:45:44.290 --> 00:45:49.360
us to inactivate and actually
modify by surgical precision,

00:45:49.360 --> 00:45:51.660
essentially any gene
of the genome in ways

00:45:51.660 --> 00:45:53.410
that we couldn't have
envisioned before.

00:45:53.410 --> 00:45:56.479
And this technology
is sweeping the field.

00:45:56.479 --> 00:45:58.270
There have been, since
these publications--

00:45:58.270 --> 00:46:01.400
which are just about three
years ago-- 2,500 papers

00:46:01.400 --> 00:46:04.326
published on this
CRISPR technology.

00:46:04.326 --> 00:46:06.200
We in the lab decided
to see if we could make

00:46:06.200 --> 00:46:07.790
this work in our mouse models.

00:46:07.790 --> 00:46:11.740
And [INAUDIBLE] and Francisco
Sanchez-Rivera and colleagues

00:46:11.740 --> 00:46:13.840
decided to use the
KP model to see

00:46:13.840 --> 00:46:15.184
whether we could make it work.

00:46:15.184 --> 00:46:17.850
And again, without going through
the details of how it was done,

00:46:17.850 --> 00:46:21.290
we created a virus that carried
the CRISPR components, in order

00:46:21.290 --> 00:46:24.610
to bring those CRISPR components
directly into the cancer cells

00:46:24.610 --> 00:46:28.210
as they were developing, and
thereby hopefully inactivate

00:46:28.210 --> 00:46:32.020
any old gene of interest, as
those cancers were evolving.

00:46:32.020 --> 00:46:35.560
And they did that with a variety
of different genes of interest.

00:46:35.560 --> 00:46:37.750
And that's shown here.

00:46:37.750 --> 00:46:39.320
And each time they
did it the cancers

00:46:39.320 --> 00:46:42.890
changed in a distinctive
and measurable way

00:46:42.890 --> 00:46:46.350
as a function of the particular
gene that we were targeting.

00:46:46.350 --> 00:46:49.510
In all in this experiment,
which took about seven months

00:46:49.510 --> 00:46:53.840
to complete, after maybe
a month of planning,

00:46:53.840 --> 00:46:56.470
we generated hundreds
of cancer samples

00:46:56.470 --> 00:47:00.300
which we could then interrogate
by various biochemical methods

00:47:00.300 --> 00:47:03.660
and study with respect to
their response to treatments.

00:47:03.660 --> 00:47:06.320
This same experiment using
the old fashioned techniques

00:47:06.320 --> 00:47:08.870
would've taken us
years to do and cost

00:47:08.870 --> 00:47:11.320
multiple hundreds of
thousands of dollars.

00:47:11.320 --> 00:47:13.150
So this has completely
changed the way

00:47:13.150 --> 00:47:16.530
that we are prosecuting genes
of interest in my lab and labs

00:47:16.530 --> 00:47:17.290
around the world.

00:47:17.290 --> 00:47:19.910
It is fast-tracking our
understanding of cancer.

00:47:19.910 --> 00:47:22.540
One of the reasons we're excited
about how quickly we will

00:47:22.540 --> 00:47:25.730
become able to come to
solutions for cancer

00:47:25.730 --> 00:47:29.147
is based on this technology.

00:47:29.147 --> 00:47:30.730
I'm going to spend
just a minute or so

00:47:30.730 --> 00:47:32.770
mentioning one
particular collaboration

00:47:32.770 --> 00:47:35.980
that involved this phase of the
process of cancer development--

00:47:35.980 --> 00:47:39.760
the metastatic phase, that
last stage, that lethal phase

00:47:39.760 --> 00:47:40.680
of cancer.

00:47:40.680 --> 00:47:42.205
We're very interested
in that phase.

00:47:42.205 --> 00:47:44.170
But I want to mention
one particular project

00:47:44.170 --> 00:47:47.330
that I'm excited about because
it underscores collaboration

00:47:47.330 --> 00:47:49.070
and technology.

00:47:49.070 --> 00:47:50.850
I was sitting around
with colleagues

00:47:50.850 --> 00:47:53.346
in the lunch line at the Koch
Institute-- Scott Manalis

00:47:53.346 --> 00:47:54.720
and Matt Vander
Heiden explaining

00:47:54.720 --> 00:47:57.980
that would be interesting if
we could build a device that we

00:47:57.980 --> 00:48:00.480
could plug into the
blood system of a mouse

00:48:00.480 --> 00:48:02.620
and see whether there
were circulating tumor

00:48:02.620 --> 00:48:06.350
cells in the blood to be able
to measure them, quantify them.

00:48:06.350 --> 00:48:09.360
To do it longitudinally to see
what the change of those cell

00:48:09.360 --> 00:48:11.190
numbers were over
time and see what

00:48:11.190 --> 00:48:13.280
their response to treatment was.

00:48:13.280 --> 00:48:15.490
This is sort of a
pie in the sky idea.

00:48:15.490 --> 00:48:20.370
But testament to the
creativity of the Koch culture,

00:48:20.370 --> 00:48:22.960
Scott Manalis said, "I
think we can do that."

00:48:22.960 --> 00:48:24.400
Sort of a can-do attitude.

00:48:24.400 --> 00:48:25.875
And then he went off and did it.

00:48:25.875 --> 00:48:27.500
And that's what you're
looking at here.

00:48:27.500 --> 00:48:30.480
This is a device which
is plugged into the blood

00:48:30.480 --> 00:48:32.920
system of a cancer-bearing
mouse that can actually

00:48:32.920 --> 00:48:36.150
measure circulating
tumor cells in real-time

00:48:36.150 --> 00:48:38.590
and longitudinally.

00:48:38.590 --> 00:48:39.530
It's not just an idea.

00:48:39.530 --> 00:48:40.760
It actually works.

00:48:40.760 --> 00:48:42.730
You can actually
see these cells.

00:48:42.730 --> 00:48:44.680
And you can actually
capture the cells

00:48:44.680 --> 00:48:46.680
by activating these
various ports.

00:48:46.680 --> 00:48:48.170
And then you can
study the biology

00:48:48.170 --> 00:48:52.470
of those cells using various
molecular techniques, including

00:48:52.470 --> 00:48:54.490
as we've done here in
Alex Shalek's lab looking

00:48:54.490 --> 00:48:56.780
at the RNA expression
of individual

00:48:56.780 --> 00:48:58.250
circulating tumor cells.

00:48:58.250 --> 00:49:01.129
This is just an example of
a technology that comes out

00:49:01.129 --> 00:49:02.920
of the kinds of
collaborations that we have

00:49:02.920 --> 00:49:06.650
available to us in the Koch.

00:49:06.650 --> 00:49:08.710
I mentioned earlier
that the interactions

00:49:08.710 --> 00:49:12.570
between cancer cells and other
cells are very important.

00:49:12.570 --> 00:49:14.780
In particular, the immune cells.

00:49:14.780 --> 00:49:17.060
Immune cells of the
body have the potential

00:49:17.060 --> 00:49:20.790
to recognize and
eliminate cancers.

00:49:20.790 --> 00:49:22.340
The hope is that
we can stimulate

00:49:22.340 --> 00:49:24.090
that to happen more effectively.

00:49:24.090 --> 00:49:26.230
And, in fact, that is
starting to happen.

00:49:26.230 --> 00:49:28.830
You might know that
cancer immunotherapy

00:49:28.830 --> 00:49:31.850
is a very powerful new
approach to the disease.

00:49:31.850 --> 00:49:35.080
Science Magazine declared
cancer immunotherapy one

00:49:35.080 --> 00:49:37.841
of the breakthroughs
of that year.

00:49:37.841 --> 00:49:39.340
And the reason is
that there are now

00:49:39.340 --> 00:49:42.460
new agents, including
FDA-approved drugs that

00:49:42.460 --> 00:49:44.910
can effectively stimulate
an immune response

00:49:44.910 --> 00:49:46.420
against your cancer.

00:49:46.420 --> 00:49:48.200
Here's an example of one.

00:49:48.200 --> 00:49:51.760
This is a study carried out
just this past year, which

00:49:51.760 --> 00:49:54.690
led to the approval of
this particular drug, which

00:49:54.690 --> 00:49:58.470
is an antibody against an
immune checkpoint called PD-1.

00:49:58.470 --> 00:50:01.020
And you can see patients
treated with this drug

00:50:01.020 --> 00:50:03.360
do much, much better
then those treated

00:50:03.360 --> 00:50:04.610
with standard chemotherapy.

00:50:04.610 --> 00:50:08.480
These patients with lung cancer
have failed previous therapy.

00:50:08.480 --> 00:50:11.970
But when treated with this
drug, even after a couple years,

00:50:11.970 --> 00:50:14.780
30% of them are alive,
compared to the control

00:50:14.780 --> 00:50:15.939
where just 10% are.

00:50:15.939 --> 00:50:18.480
And we know from other studies
that these patients are likely

00:50:18.480 --> 00:50:21.560
going to have durable
cures, or durable responses

00:50:21.560 --> 00:50:23.124
in some cases-- cures.

00:50:23.124 --> 00:50:24.540
So these drugs
have really started

00:50:24.540 --> 00:50:26.456
to change the way we
think about our prospects

00:50:26.456 --> 00:50:30.750
for controlling cancer by
stimulating the immune cells.

00:50:30.750 --> 00:50:32.050
The action is right here.

00:50:32.050 --> 00:50:33.650
There are T-cells
cells in your body

00:50:33.650 --> 00:50:36.060
they can attack foreign cells.

00:50:36.060 --> 00:50:38.730
And your cells, your cancer
cells become foreign.

00:50:38.730 --> 00:50:41.890
If you can stimulate T-cells
to attack your cancer cells,

00:50:41.890 --> 00:50:43.390
that's a good thing.

00:50:43.390 --> 00:50:44.900
So we got excited
about the prospect

00:50:44.900 --> 00:50:48.140
of studying the tumor immune
interface with our mouse

00:50:48.140 --> 00:50:49.570
models.

00:50:49.570 --> 00:50:52.110
And so Michael [INAUDIBLE]
and Anne Chung and others

00:50:52.110 --> 00:50:55.340
built models to stimulate
immune responses against cancer.

00:50:55.340 --> 00:50:58.960
John Proctor funded
some of that early work.

00:50:58.960 --> 00:51:02.040
This was done by
manipulating those viruses

00:51:02.040 --> 00:51:04.920
that we use to start the
process at the beginning.

00:51:04.920 --> 00:51:06.290
And this actually does work.

00:51:06.290 --> 00:51:10.250
If you introduce into
these tumors antigens

00:51:10.250 --> 00:51:13.430
that the immune system can see,
you can attract immune cells

00:51:13.430 --> 00:51:14.960
to the tumors.

00:51:14.960 --> 00:51:17.240
You can actually get
T-cells into the tumor

00:51:17.240 --> 00:51:18.360
micro-environment.

00:51:18.360 --> 00:51:19.890
And they're not just bystanders.

00:51:19.890 --> 00:51:21.264
They're actually
doing something.

00:51:21.264 --> 00:51:23.910
You can measure an
anti-tumor response

00:51:23.910 --> 00:51:25.970
by those infiltrating T-cells.

00:51:25.970 --> 00:51:27.680
They're attacking the
tumor just like we

00:51:27.680 --> 00:51:30.980
want them to do, for a while.

00:51:30.980 --> 00:51:33.780
But eventually, and not
very long thereafter,

00:51:33.780 --> 00:51:37.260
they fail to sustain
that response.

00:51:37.260 --> 00:51:40.390
The number of those
tumor-reactive T-cells drops.

00:51:40.390 --> 00:51:42.200
Their activity states drop.

00:51:42.200 --> 00:51:46.890
They become-- and this is a
technical term-- exhausted.

00:51:46.890 --> 00:51:49.110
They're there, but
they're not functioning.

00:51:49.110 --> 00:51:52.520
And so we decided we would
investigate this process.

00:51:52.520 --> 00:51:55.920
And various investigators--
Michael, Amy Lee, Nik Joshi

00:51:55.920 --> 00:51:58.550
and others have looked
at the system in our lab.

00:51:58.550 --> 00:52:01.980
There are various ways that the
immune system can get shut off

00:52:01.980 --> 00:52:03.240
in the development of cancer.

00:52:03.240 --> 00:52:04.760
Here's a list of them.

00:52:04.760 --> 00:52:07.150
These checkpoints get activated.

00:52:07.150 --> 00:52:08.970
Various cells of the
micro-environment

00:52:08.970 --> 00:52:12.355
turn off immune function.

00:52:12.355 --> 00:52:14.480
One of those checkpoints
that I actually mentioned,

00:52:14.480 --> 00:52:19.760
PD-1, does in fact get
up-regulated in the T-cells

00:52:19.760 --> 00:52:21.480
at that late time point.

00:52:21.480 --> 00:52:24.690
But Amy Lee has actually
tried that anti-PD-1 antibody

00:52:24.690 --> 00:52:26.620
and it doesn't work
in this system.

00:52:26.620 --> 00:52:29.840
So this is a challenge to
understand why it doesn't work,

00:52:29.840 --> 00:52:31.625
and what we might be
able to do about it.

00:52:31.625 --> 00:52:34.420
Nik Joshi has asked
whether a different cell,

00:52:34.420 --> 00:52:37.100
a different mechanism,
in this case a cell

00:52:37.100 --> 00:52:39.530
called a regulatory
T-cell might be

00:52:39.530 --> 00:52:42.200
involved in turning
off the immune response

00:52:42.200 --> 00:52:43.760
in an established tumor.

00:52:43.760 --> 00:52:48.000
First he measured the quantity
of these regulatory T-cells

00:52:48.000 --> 00:52:50.380
in the tumor and found that
they were, in fact, highly

00:52:50.380 --> 00:52:51.470
increased.

00:52:51.470 --> 00:52:53.990
In tumor-bearing mice
at this late stage

00:52:53.990 --> 00:52:57.920
is about a 20-fold increase in
T-regulatory cells in the tumor

00:52:57.920 --> 00:53:00.680
micro-environment at the time
the T-cells get exhausted.

00:53:00.680 --> 00:53:02.640
That's guilt by association.

00:53:02.640 --> 00:53:04.920
But in these mouse models
we can do experiments.

00:53:04.920 --> 00:53:07.597
And Nik was able to deplete
the T-regulatory cells

00:53:07.597 --> 00:53:09.430
and ask what the
consequences of getting rid

00:53:09.430 --> 00:53:12.320
of that inhibitory
cell type was.

00:53:12.320 --> 00:53:14.180
This is the before picture.

00:53:14.180 --> 00:53:16.190
The tumor cells are
nice and juicy red.

00:53:16.190 --> 00:53:20.160
The T-cells in green are sitting
on the sidelines exhausted,

00:53:20.160 --> 00:53:22.560
not controlling the tumor.

00:53:22.560 --> 00:53:24.600
He could then get rid of
the T-regulatory cells

00:53:24.600 --> 00:53:25.750
by this method.

00:53:25.750 --> 00:53:28.930
And when he did so he saw this.

00:53:28.930 --> 00:53:31.220
The immune cells now
increase the number

00:53:31.220 --> 00:53:33.320
and infiltrated into the tumor.

00:53:33.320 --> 00:53:37.320
And when they did so they caused
massive tumor destruction.

00:53:37.320 --> 00:53:40.140
So T-regulatory cells are
controlling an immune response

00:53:40.140 --> 00:53:43.124
in this model system, likely
in human cancers as well,

00:53:43.124 --> 00:53:44.540
and we're now
trying to figure out

00:53:44.540 --> 00:53:48.430
ways of specifically
inactivating regulatory T-cells

00:53:48.430 --> 00:53:51.000
in the tumor micro-environment
to be able to translate

00:53:51.000 --> 00:53:54.410
this for use in people one day.

00:53:54.410 --> 00:53:54.910
All right.

00:53:54.910 --> 00:53:56.489
I'm coming to the
end of the hour.

00:53:56.489 --> 00:53:59.030
I just want to mention two very
brief projects, both of which

00:53:59.030 --> 00:54:03.690
are nascent projects in the
lab, a view towards the future,

00:54:03.690 --> 00:54:06.870
and also underscore the
collaborations that we have

00:54:06.870 --> 00:54:09.690
available to us at the Koch.

00:54:09.690 --> 00:54:11.900
And the first involves
a collaboration

00:54:11.900 --> 00:54:14.480
with Sangeeta Bhatia's
lab on this process

00:54:14.480 --> 00:54:15.690
of early detection.

00:54:15.690 --> 00:54:20.340
If we could eliminate cancers
at a very early stage, when

00:54:20.340 --> 00:54:22.900
they're, for example,
surgically respectable,

00:54:22.900 --> 00:54:24.590
our success rates
would go way up.

00:54:24.590 --> 00:54:27.740
But we need to be able to
find those cancers earlier.

00:54:27.740 --> 00:54:31.580
And Sangeeta's lab is developing
nanotechnology-based strategies

00:54:31.580 --> 00:54:32.399
to do that.

00:54:32.399 --> 00:54:33.940
And they published
some of this work,

00:54:33.940 --> 00:54:36.080
but not as it
relates to the lungs.

00:54:36.080 --> 00:54:37.990
So this is unpublished data.

00:54:37.990 --> 00:54:40.770
And what they do is to take
an aerosolized nanoparticle

00:54:40.770 --> 00:54:44.000
and introduce it into ultimately
humans-- in these experiments

00:54:44.000 --> 00:54:49.460
mice-- into the lungs of
tumor-bearing or non-animals.

00:54:49.460 --> 00:54:52.010
These nanoparticles have
little tags on them,

00:54:52.010 --> 00:54:53.350
shown in red here.

00:54:53.350 --> 00:54:55.300
And these tags
can be clipped off

00:54:55.300 --> 00:54:58.180
by an enzyme-- this little
Pac-Man thing-- an enzyme that

00:54:58.180 --> 00:55:02.210
gets up-regulated in the
cancers specifically.

00:55:02.210 --> 00:55:03.970
If the tags get
clipped off, they

00:55:03.970 --> 00:55:06.840
can now get excreted
into the urine

00:55:06.840 --> 00:55:08.420
and detected in the urine.

00:55:08.420 --> 00:55:10.600
And then by a simple
urine test one

00:55:10.600 --> 00:55:14.640
can find the evidence
of a tumor in the lung.

00:55:14.640 --> 00:55:19.265
Seems crazy, but actually, in a
collaboration involving Andrew

00:55:19.265 --> 00:55:22.810
Warrem and [INAUDIBLE]-- they
tested this idea by allowing

00:55:22.810 --> 00:55:27.280
mice with these lung tumors
to inhale the nanoparticles,

00:55:27.280 --> 00:55:29.270
and then tested them,
either at three weeks,

00:55:29.270 --> 00:55:32.450
when the tumors are tiny, or
at six weeks or at seven weeks

00:55:32.450 --> 00:55:36.910
to see whether this was a signal
of nanoparticle activation.

00:55:39.430 --> 00:55:41.295
And lo and behold,
at three weeks

00:55:41.295 --> 00:55:43.170
there's not much of a
signal but at six weeks

00:55:43.170 --> 00:55:44.200
you can clearly see it.

00:55:44.200 --> 00:55:47.520
At seven weeks it's
undeniable that you

00:55:47.520 --> 00:55:49.870
can detect cancers in
these mice by this very

00:55:49.870 --> 00:55:54.070
simple nanoparticle-based
aerosolized urine test.

00:55:54.070 --> 00:55:56.530
I should mention that we make
the undergraduates collect

00:55:56.530 --> 00:55:57.240
the urine.

00:55:57.240 --> 00:55:58.720
[LAUGHTER]

00:55:58.720 --> 00:56:01.340
Kidding about that.

00:56:01.340 --> 00:56:04.960
So I'm excited about early
detection opportunities.

00:56:04.960 --> 00:56:08.697
And finally, I'm excited about
prevention opportunities.

00:56:08.697 --> 00:56:10.280
I think among the
projects in the lab,

00:56:10.280 --> 00:56:11.770
I'm excited about all of them.

00:56:11.770 --> 00:56:13.436
I'm probably most
excited about the idea

00:56:13.436 --> 00:56:16.517
that we can actually
prevent cancers one day.

00:56:16.517 --> 00:56:18.100
And this a project
that's in the hands

00:56:18.100 --> 00:56:20.740
of David Cantor in my
lab, in collaboration

00:56:20.740 --> 00:56:25.320
Omar Khan and [INAUDIBLE] who
work in the Anderson and Langer

00:56:25.320 --> 00:56:27.664
labs in the Koch Institute.

00:56:27.664 --> 00:56:29.080
And the idea, which
I'm just going

00:56:29.080 --> 00:56:31.670
to give you in concept
form, is that we've

00:56:31.670 --> 00:56:35.110
learned a lot about how these
tumors evolve in this mouse

00:56:35.110 --> 00:56:36.390
model.

00:56:36.390 --> 00:56:38.740
And there's one particular
protein shown here

00:56:38.740 --> 00:56:42.110
called Hmga2, which
gets regularly

00:56:42.110 --> 00:56:44.910
up-regulated in
late stage cancers.

00:56:44.910 --> 00:56:47.200
It's not there in the
early stage tumors.

00:56:47.200 --> 00:56:48.750
But as shown in
the brown staining

00:56:48.750 --> 00:56:50.730
it becomes expressed
in more than half

00:56:50.730 --> 00:56:51.994
of these late stage tumors.

00:56:51.994 --> 00:56:53.660
Now the important
thing you need to know

00:56:53.660 --> 00:56:57.400
is that Hmga2 is not
normally expressed anywhere

00:56:57.400 --> 00:57:00.040
in the adult animal.

00:57:00.040 --> 00:57:03.100
It's only expressed
in the embryo.

00:57:03.100 --> 00:57:06.750
But it becomes abnormally
up-regulated in the tumor.

00:57:06.750 --> 00:57:10.550
And so the question is,
if we know that's coming,

00:57:10.550 --> 00:57:13.690
can we vaccinate
the mouse such that

00:57:13.690 --> 00:57:17.330
when it sees that it will
eliminate those cancer cells?

00:57:17.330 --> 00:57:19.990
Prophylactic vaccination,
preventative vaccination

00:57:19.990 --> 00:57:22.130
based on our knowledge
of what's coming,

00:57:22.130 --> 00:57:26.770
just like vaccination
against infectious disease.

00:57:26.770 --> 00:57:29.470
And so David and
his collaborators

00:57:29.470 --> 00:57:32.530
are now doing the experiment
of taking the KP mouse

00:57:32.530 --> 00:57:35.900
and vaccinating it with
this protein, Hmga2.

00:57:35.900 --> 00:57:39.090
So we'll get an immune
response against Hmga2.

00:57:39.090 --> 00:57:41.910
And then we would induce
tumors in these mice,

00:57:41.910 --> 00:57:44.910
and ask whether, in
fact, we can protect them

00:57:44.910 --> 00:57:47.130
against the development
of advanced cancers.

00:57:47.130 --> 00:57:49.430
If this works it
will suggest that we

00:57:49.430 --> 00:57:51.620
could do similar
things in human beings,

00:57:51.620 --> 00:57:53.670
and one day perhaps we will.

00:57:53.670 --> 00:57:55.490
And if you like that
idea, I encourage

00:57:55.490 --> 00:57:58.680
you to consider coming
to our symposium in June

00:57:58.680 --> 00:58:02.586
on the subject of prevention
and early detection of cancer.

00:58:02.586 --> 00:58:04.210
So I've come up to
the end of the hour.

00:58:04.210 --> 00:58:07.670
I'm going to thank--
just two more slides.

00:58:07.670 --> 00:58:10.500
The work that I described to you
requires tremendous resources

00:58:10.500 --> 00:58:12.640
and I've received tremendous
resources and support

00:58:12.640 --> 00:58:15.020
from many places, including
importantly the Howard Hughes

00:58:15.020 --> 00:58:16.610
Medical Institute,
the National Cancer

00:58:16.610 --> 00:58:19.870
Institute, the Lustgarten
Foundation, tremendous support

00:58:19.870 --> 00:58:21.800
from the Koch
Institute including

00:58:21.800 --> 00:58:24.570
the Swanson Biotechnology
Center and other funders.

00:58:24.570 --> 00:58:27.220
And finally I'm going
to stop where I started.

00:58:27.220 --> 00:58:28.774
Nothing I presented
to you could not

00:58:28.774 --> 00:58:31.190
have been done by the collective
efforts of a tremendously

00:58:31.190 --> 00:58:32.770
talented group of people.

00:58:32.770 --> 00:58:35.600
And I want to thank them,
thank you, and thank you again

00:58:35.600 --> 00:58:37.598
for the Killian award.

00:58:37.598 --> 00:58:41.590
[APPLAUSE]

00:58:58.930 --> 00:59:01.172
Thank you very much Tyler.

00:59:01.172 --> 00:59:02.880
I would ask people
with questions-- there

00:59:02.880 --> 00:59:04.450
are microphones in both aisles.

00:59:04.450 --> 00:59:06.260
There's one here and
there's one somewhere

00:59:06.260 --> 00:59:08.190
right about where I'm
pointing there-- please

00:59:08.190 --> 00:59:12.860
come to the microphones
and Tyler will be

00:59:12.860 --> 00:59:14.940
happy to take some questions.

00:59:14.940 --> 00:59:19.300
And if you prefer to shout, I'm
happy to repeat your question.

00:59:19.300 --> 00:59:21.420
This side.

00:59:21.420 --> 00:59:25.120
Can you tell us
whether or not cancers

00:59:25.120 --> 00:59:28.580
are at all found in embryos?

00:59:28.580 --> 00:59:32.340
And if not, is that
significant and does

00:59:32.340 --> 00:59:34.016
that tell one anything?

00:59:34.016 --> 00:59:36.540
The question is, are
cancers in embryos?

00:59:36.540 --> 00:59:38.870
Occasionally yes-- occasionally.

00:59:38.870 --> 00:59:41.670
But if you recall my earlier
comments about the fact

00:59:41.670 --> 00:59:45.160
that cancers require the
acquisition of alterations

00:59:45.160 --> 00:59:48.010
in genes, it's not surprising
that you wouldn't find them

00:59:48.010 --> 00:59:51.170
very often, because
of the opportunity

00:59:51.170 --> 00:59:55.330
for an embryonic cell to
acquire the necessary changes

00:59:55.330 --> 00:59:59.080
to convert it from normalcy to
malignancy is very, very low.

00:59:59.080 --> 01:00:02.500
It typically requires a long
time, years or even decades

01:00:02.500 --> 01:00:04.390
to acquire the necessary damage.

01:00:04.390 --> 01:00:06.940
At some frequency that
will happen in short term,

01:00:06.940 --> 01:00:09.770
and so we do occasionally
see it, including in embryos

01:00:09.770 --> 01:00:11.540
sometimes in
children as you know.

01:00:11.540 --> 01:00:15.054
But those disease
are relatively rare.

01:00:15.054 --> 01:00:17.220
If you're far away from a
mic, just raise your hand.

01:00:20.830 --> 01:00:23.180
So as you know,
this immunotherapy

01:00:23.180 --> 01:00:27.410
which is so exciting,
because it gives some cures,

01:00:27.410 --> 01:00:31.070
doesn't work in a number
of cancer so far--

01:00:31.070 --> 01:00:35.770
breast cancer, some hint in
prostate, but in many others

01:00:35.770 --> 01:00:37.490
there's no hint at all.

01:00:37.490 --> 01:00:43.170
Do you see the same sort of
Treg effects in those tumors

01:00:43.170 --> 01:00:44.561
as you do in your model?

01:00:44.561 --> 01:00:45.060
Yeah.

01:00:45.060 --> 01:00:47.610
So the question is
a good one, Phil.

01:00:47.610 --> 01:00:49.680
It relates to our
lack of understanding

01:00:49.680 --> 01:00:52.890
of why immunotherapy works when
it works and not otherwise.

01:00:52.890 --> 01:00:55.050
So you're asking whether
Tregs might contribute.

01:00:55.050 --> 01:00:57.410
I think the answer
is likely yes.

01:00:57.410 --> 01:01:00.600
Certainly there are cancer
epidemiological studies

01:01:00.600 --> 01:01:04.410
that would suggest that
the presence of Tregs

01:01:04.410 --> 01:01:06.280
correlates with
poorer prognosis,

01:01:06.280 --> 01:01:08.990
and that would suggest the Tregs
may be inhibiting an otherwise

01:01:08.990 --> 01:01:10.174
effective immune response.

01:01:10.174 --> 01:01:11.590
I think that's
part of the answer.

01:01:11.590 --> 01:01:12.881
There are likely other answers.

01:01:12.881 --> 01:01:15.870
Why, in certain circumstances
the immune system can see

01:01:15.870 --> 01:01:17.750
a tumor, and in
other cases it can't?

01:01:17.750 --> 01:01:21.080
The immune system relies
on there being differences

01:01:21.080 --> 01:01:22.720
between tumor and normal.

01:01:22.720 --> 01:01:24.610
The more differences the better.

01:01:24.610 --> 01:01:28.270
Lung cancer, where this
treatment works-- melanoma,

01:01:28.270 --> 01:01:30.140
where this treatment
works, happen

01:01:30.140 --> 01:01:33.470
to be two of the tumors that
are full of mutations, because

01:01:33.470 --> 01:01:36.830
of the carcinogens of
smoke or of the sun.

01:01:36.830 --> 01:01:38.150
So they're very different.

01:01:38.150 --> 01:01:40.030
And that might be why the
immune system is particularly

01:01:40.030 --> 01:01:40.990
good at seeing them.

01:01:40.990 --> 01:01:43.239
But I think some of these
other suppressive mechanisms

01:01:43.239 --> 01:01:45.940
are likely part of
the answer as well.

01:01:45.940 --> 01:01:48.120
Yes, I speak to
you-- is this on?

01:01:48.120 --> 01:01:48.830
Is it working?

01:01:48.830 --> 01:01:49.330
Yes.

01:01:49.330 --> 01:01:50.520
Oh.

01:01:50.520 --> 01:01:52.720
--not as a biologist
but as an engineer,

01:01:52.720 --> 01:01:56.660
and a recent pancreatic
cancer survivor.

01:01:56.660 --> 01:02:02.170
Recently the vice president
announced a moonshot approach.

01:02:02.170 --> 01:02:05.740
In reading the literature in
the popular press, et cetera,

01:02:05.740 --> 01:02:10.460
I see a lot of negativity about
the approach to this moonshot,

01:02:10.460 --> 01:02:14.370
namely that cancer is more
than 200 separate diseases

01:02:14.370 --> 01:02:17.500
and largely unique
to the individual.

01:02:17.500 --> 01:02:19.250
Can you give us a
comment on how this

01:02:19.250 --> 01:02:21.930
is going to affect the future?

01:02:21.930 --> 01:02:22.430
Yeah.

01:02:22.430 --> 01:02:24.950
So I think that,
for one, we don't

01:02:24.950 --> 01:02:28.310
have a very clear understanding
of what the moonshot

01:02:28.310 --> 01:02:30.240
effort will look like.

01:02:30.240 --> 01:02:33.140
We hope that it comes with
a significant increase

01:02:33.140 --> 01:02:34.070
in funding.

01:02:34.070 --> 01:02:38.350
And that will allow a variety
of things to be tried and tested

01:02:38.350 --> 01:02:39.470
and accelerated.

01:02:39.470 --> 01:02:40.870
So I think the
details still have

01:02:40.870 --> 01:02:43.620
to come in to give you a
proper answer to your question.

01:02:43.620 --> 01:02:47.390
I think some of the negativity
relates to our general concern

01:02:47.390 --> 01:02:49.580
in the cancer research
community of trying

01:02:49.580 --> 01:02:51.180
to put a timeline on anything.

01:02:51.180 --> 01:02:52.480
These are hard problems.

01:02:52.480 --> 01:02:55.660
And it's difficult to
say, with more money

01:02:55.660 --> 01:02:58.444
we'll solve the problem
in two years or three.

01:02:58.444 --> 01:02:59.860
Those things have
been made before

01:02:59.860 --> 01:03:03.170
and I think they are unwise
and not very helpful.

01:03:03.170 --> 01:03:06.930
So more funding and dedicated
efforts in certain areas--

01:03:06.930 --> 01:03:08.660
immunotherapy's on that list.

01:03:08.660 --> 01:03:10.910
Immunotherapy's very
promising, but we

01:03:10.910 --> 01:03:13.057
have questions still to answer.

01:03:13.057 --> 01:03:14.640
With more funding
and concerted effort

01:03:14.640 --> 01:03:16.380
to understand some
of those questions,

01:03:16.380 --> 01:03:19.190
will you be in a better place
to use those treatments,

01:03:19.190 --> 01:03:20.780
maybe even in the short term.

01:03:20.780 --> 01:03:23.360
Other things on that list are
early detection, I mentioned,

01:03:23.360 --> 01:03:25.640
even prevention I mentioned.

01:03:25.640 --> 01:03:28.416
Those areas don't get
enough funding right now.

01:03:28.416 --> 01:03:30.040
And so maybe with
some additional funds

01:03:30.040 --> 01:03:33.190
we can shift the curve
of diagnosis, or even

01:03:33.190 --> 01:03:34.230
to prevention.

01:03:34.230 --> 01:03:36.180
So I'm not a skeptic.

01:03:36.180 --> 01:03:38.000
I'm an optimist.

01:03:38.000 --> 01:03:39.640
And I'm happy that
the vice president

01:03:39.640 --> 01:03:40.889
is talking about more funding.

01:03:40.889 --> 01:03:44.170
That's always a good thing.

01:03:44.170 --> 01:03:46.350
Do I see further hands?

01:03:46.350 --> 01:03:47.338
Yes.

01:03:47.338 --> 01:03:49.290
I guess one of the
cancer obviously

01:03:49.290 --> 01:03:51.242
is that it's a
disease of the self.

01:03:51.242 --> 01:03:53.682
And the immune system goes
through a [INAUDIBLE] selection

01:03:53.682 --> 01:03:55.634
process so that you [INAUDIBLE].

01:03:55.634 --> 01:03:59.050
So regarding this last
vaccine trial effort,

01:03:59.050 --> 01:04:00.942
do you have any
experience, for instance,

01:04:00.942 --> 01:04:03.442
indicating at which point do
tumors acquire immunoresistance

01:04:03.442 --> 01:04:04.418
or [INAUDIBLE]?

01:04:04.418 --> 01:04:08.728
And is this potential, even
when an earlier vaccine

01:04:08.728 --> 01:04:13.670
is delivered for a tumor to
form and maintain a [INAUDIBLE]?

01:04:13.670 --> 01:04:14.790
Hmga2.

01:04:14.790 --> 01:04:17.855
So what I showed
you is a concept.

01:04:17.855 --> 01:04:20.110
It's in very early stages.

01:04:20.110 --> 01:04:24.320
You raised the question of self
versus non-self and tolerance.

01:04:24.320 --> 01:04:27.395
We actually believe that we can
create a vaccine against Hmga2.

01:04:27.395 --> 01:04:29.270
I won't talk about those
data because they're

01:04:29.270 --> 01:04:30.228
very, very preliminary.

01:04:30.228 --> 01:04:32.720
But I think it's possible
that we can at least get

01:04:32.720 --> 01:04:35.790
the immune system to see Hmga2.

01:04:35.790 --> 01:04:41.290
If we can, then the tumor will
be in an ongoing discussion

01:04:41.290 --> 01:04:42.569
with the immune system.

01:04:42.569 --> 01:04:44.110
Sometimes the immune
system will win,

01:04:44.110 --> 01:04:45.734
and I think that
would be a good thing.

01:04:45.734 --> 01:04:47.100
Sometimes the tumor will win.

01:04:47.100 --> 01:04:50.210
And some of these
immuno-evasion mechanisms, Tregs

01:04:50.210 --> 01:04:52.120
and other things
will come into play.

01:04:52.120 --> 01:04:56.040
Maybe the tumors will lose
the expiration of Hmga2.

01:04:56.040 --> 01:04:59.180
But sometimes they will
see a positive effect,

01:04:59.180 --> 01:05:03.560
and that might lead to a
shift in the likelihood

01:05:03.560 --> 01:05:05.430
that somebody who's,
for example predisposed

01:05:05.430 --> 01:05:08.170
to particular cancer will
get it or how long it

01:05:08.170 --> 01:05:09.410
will take them to get it.

01:05:09.410 --> 01:05:10.830
So these might not be panaceas.

01:05:10.830 --> 01:05:13.510
They may not solve the
problem for the reasons

01:05:13.510 --> 01:05:17.100
that you're suggesting, but
I think they might well help,

01:05:17.100 --> 01:05:20.280
and that's why
we're pursuing it.

01:05:20.280 --> 01:05:22.420
So I'll ask a question
myself while I'm

01:05:22.420 --> 01:05:23.790
looking for further hands.

01:05:23.790 --> 01:05:26.090
So I see many in
the room whom I take

01:05:26.090 --> 01:05:28.660
to be students and post-docs.

01:05:28.660 --> 01:05:31.210
So thinking of all the students
and post-docs in the room,

01:05:31.210 --> 01:05:33.440
could you sort of look
back on your own experience

01:05:33.440 --> 01:05:35.340
and talk about how you
picked good problems

01:05:35.340 --> 01:05:38.520
or how they find you?

01:05:38.520 --> 01:05:40.710
Well, what I tell my
students and post-docs

01:05:40.710 --> 01:05:43.210
is a little bit about my comment
about swimming in your lane

01:05:43.210 --> 01:05:44.420
and keeping your head down.

01:05:44.420 --> 01:05:46.650
I think it's good
to keep your head up

01:05:46.650 --> 01:05:48.660
and swim out of your lane.

01:05:48.660 --> 01:05:51.450
I think it's good to go
beyond, to challenge yourself

01:05:51.450 --> 01:05:52.950
and to push beyond
the conventional.

01:05:52.950 --> 01:05:54.920
So my students and
post-docs do things

01:05:54.920 --> 01:05:57.080
that people have never
tried to do before.

01:05:57.080 --> 01:06:00.650
And sometimes it's hard
and it takes a long time,

01:06:00.650 --> 01:06:02.560
but usually with a good
idea and perseverance

01:06:02.560 --> 01:06:03.518
you can get to the end.

01:06:03.518 --> 01:06:06.279
So challenging yourself to
doing something difficult

01:06:06.279 --> 01:06:07.195
is part of the answer.

01:06:10.450 --> 01:06:10.950
Yeah.

01:06:10.950 --> 01:06:15.460
I think in addition, just
related to the Koch concept,

01:06:15.460 --> 01:06:19.400
keeping your mind open
to alternative approaches

01:06:19.400 --> 01:06:20.220
from other fields.

01:06:20.220 --> 01:06:22.000
I think I'm seeing
this more and more,

01:06:22.000 --> 01:06:24.330
and I've given you
some examples, where

01:06:24.330 --> 01:06:26.810
if you just focus
on what you know,

01:06:26.810 --> 01:06:30.180
your blinding yourself to
many new opportunities.

01:06:30.180 --> 01:06:31.420
Thank you.

01:06:31.420 --> 01:06:33.875
Right in the middle there
and then I'll go back there.

01:06:33.875 --> 01:06:34.375
Yes.

01:06:34.375 --> 01:06:36.640
Looking at the
circulating tumor cells

01:06:36.640 --> 01:06:41.590
that you discussed, and next to
sequencing and ultra-sensitive

01:06:41.590 --> 01:06:43.735
[INAUDIBLE] from
DNA, do you think

01:06:43.735 --> 01:06:46.540
it will be feasible
in the near future

01:06:46.540 --> 01:06:50.500
to basically screen [INAUDIBLE]
cancers using these methods?

01:06:50.500 --> 01:06:51.250
Yeah.

01:06:51.250 --> 01:06:53.110
It's a great question.

01:06:53.110 --> 01:06:57.850
Circulating tumor cells-- the
answer is likely yes, even now.

01:06:57.850 --> 01:07:00.240
These cells are being
used to track the state

01:07:00.240 --> 01:07:02.690
of an individual's tumor, now.

01:07:02.690 --> 01:07:06.140
How many there are pre,
post-treatment for example.

01:07:06.140 --> 01:07:10.480
Soon the circulating
DNA that you referenced

01:07:10.480 --> 01:07:11.900
will be used in a
similar fashion.

01:07:11.900 --> 01:07:14.000
In fact it already is
being used in that way.

01:07:14.000 --> 01:07:16.850
And maybe before too
long, circulating DNA

01:07:16.850 --> 01:07:19.450
will be used to test
whether somebody's

01:07:19.450 --> 01:07:23.114
got an early staged cancer in
the form of early detection.

01:07:23.114 --> 01:07:24.780
And one of our speakers
at our symposium

01:07:24.780 --> 01:07:27.780
actually, Bert Vogelstein will
talk about that very approach.

01:07:27.780 --> 01:07:29.540
So I think the answer is yes.

01:07:29.540 --> 01:07:31.380
You worry a little bit
about what will you

01:07:31.380 --> 01:07:34.220
detect, because if you
have an abnormal cell,

01:07:34.220 --> 01:07:35.830
it's not yet a cancer.

01:07:35.830 --> 01:07:38.974
Do you really want to know
that and intervene then?

01:07:38.974 --> 01:07:40.640
So we need a little
bit more specificity

01:07:40.640 --> 01:07:42.760
about what the
signal is telling us.

01:07:42.760 --> 01:07:44.190
But the answer is, I think, yes.

01:07:44.190 --> 01:07:47.540
Before long these tests will
be used, perhaps routinely.

01:07:47.540 --> 01:07:50.110
Your annual checkup
could include sequencing

01:07:50.110 --> 01:07:51.580
of your circulating DNA.

01:07:51.580 --> 01:07:53.976
In fact, I'll bet on it.

01:07:53.976 --> 01:07:57.210
And there was a
question right there.

01:07:57.210 --> 01:07:57.710
[INAUDIBLE]

01:08:13.269 --> 01:08:14.560
I'm sorry can you say it again.

01:08:14.560 --> 01:08:16.059
I didn't-- I didn't
hear the middle.

01:08:16.059 --> 01:08:18.871
It's K-ras and p53, yes?

01:08:18.871 --> 01:08:19.371
[INAUDIBLE]

01:08:26.740 --> 01:08:28.460
Yeah.

01:08:28.460 --> 01:08:29.200
Yeah.

01:08:29.200 --> 01:08:31.770
So there's a detailed
question related

01:08:31.770 --> 01:08:35.390
to the specific mechanisms
of activation or inactivation

01:08:35.390 --> 01:08:37.830
of K-ras and p53.

01:08:37.830 --> 01:08:41.430
Short answer to your question
is yes, there are differences.

01:08:41.430 --> 01:08:45.840
So the exact way in
which you activate K-ras

01:08:45.840 --> 01:08:48.870
changes its effect on the cell.

01:08:48.870 --> 01:08:52.149
So different tumors do
it slightly differently.

01:08:52.149 --> 01:08:53.773
Two tumors of the
same type that do it

01:08:53.773 --> 01:08:55.189
with slightly
different mechanisms

01:08:55.189 --> 01:08:56.569
can have different responses.

01:08:56.569 --> 01:08:58.560
And P53 is similar actually.

01:08:58.560 --> 01:09:00.510
The way that you
inactivate it can

01:09:00.510 --> 01:09:04.174
influence the exact pathways
that cell will take.

01:09:04.174 --> 01:09:06.560
Are there other questions?

01:09:06.560 --> 01:09:07.100
Right here.

01:09:07.100 --> 01:09:08.412
Yes.

01:09:08.412 --> 01:09:10.027
There are many,
many different kinds

01:09:10.027 --> 01:09:12.773
of mice that have
been developed.

01:09:12.773 --> 01:09:14.189
Many, many different
kinds of mice

01:09:14.189 --> 01:09:17.270
that have been developed
in your laboratory.

01:09:17.270 --> 01:09:20.205
And if that's the case
then it seems to me

01:09:20.205 --> 01:09:23.200
that there are many different
ways then that present

01:09:23.200 --> 01:09:27.020
themselves as to how
to treat these mice,

01:09:27.020 --> 01:09:31.310
and therefore develop a much
greater likelihood that you

01:09:31.310 --> 01:09:33.210
can cure those kinds of mice.

01:09:33.210 --> 01:09:34.020
Yeah.

01:09:34.020 --> 01:09:36.290
Well that was one of
the motivations, David,

01:09:36.290 --> 01:09:38.010
for creating these models.

01:09:38.010 --> 01:09:41.189
We needed to have better
systems that we believed in,

01:09:41.189 --> 01:09:43.680
that were faithful
to the human disease.

01:09:43.680 --> 01:09:47.307
And the answer is that
now that we have them,

01:09:47.307 --> 01:09:52.350
the treatments that we
can test, the likelihood

01:09:52.350 --> 01:09:54.950
that the results that
we see in those models

01:09:54.950 --> 01:09:56.870
will play out
eventually clinically

01:09:56.870 --> 01:09:57.840
is much, much greater.

01:09:57.840 --> 01:09:59.340
So we have much greater faith.

01:09:59.340 --> 01:10:02.200
And that's really important
because right now there are too

01:10:02.200 --> 01:10:04.910
many drugs to test in patients.

01:10:04.910 --> 01:10:06.590
Phil mentioned immunotherapy.

01:10:06.590 --> 01:10:09.390
There are so many
candidates and combinations

01:10:09.390 --> 01:10:11.280
that could be
tested, it outstrips

01:10:11.280 --> 01:10:14.200
the number of patients
that could be tested with.

01:10:14.200 --> 01:10:15.450
And so we need better systems.

01:10:15.450 --> 01:10:17.325
And that was actually
the motivation way back

01:10:17.325 --> 01:10:21.120
when to create this model so we
can create predictive responses

01:10:21.120 --> 01:10:23.400
for treatment to help
guide us to figure out what

01:10:23.400 --> 01:10:26.357
to use eventually in patients.

01:10:26.357 --> 01:10:27.190
Thank you very much.

01:10:27.190 --> 01:10:29.370
Let me ask everyone to
join me in thanking you

01:10:29.370 --> 01:10:30.800
for a wonderful lecture.

01:10:30.800 --> 01:10:33.500
[APPLAUSE]

01:10:33.500 --> 01:10:35.900
And congratulations again.

01:10:35.900 --> 01:10:39.850
[APPLAUSE]

